//  || 26438 THE CONSTANT BIAS FLOOR IS fp32 WORLD PRECISION, NOT DEPTH-BUFFER PRECISION (full ledger at KH_WORLD_ULP_FLOOR; 235 restores the D32 floor). FIELD ON 26437: z-fighting mostly gone but surviving as isolated dark TRIANGLES on faces that are almost DIRECTLY LIT - the opposite end of the incidence range from everything since 26422, which names the term immediately: at normal incidence tan -> 1, the SLOPE contributes nothing, and only the CONSTANT floor holds the compare apart. 26436 dropped the shader slope 3.0 -> 0.35 and took the constant with it, because the constant was riding on that term's tan = 1 minimum - 26431 gave 0.245 + 3.00*texelWorld = 1.714 mm (no acne), 26437 gives 0.245 + 0.35*texelWorld = 0.416 mm (acne). AND THE THRESHOLD BETWEEN THEM IS NOT ARBITRARY: it is ONE ULP OF THE WORLD COORDINATE. |wpos| is ~7069 m here and fp32 ulp there is 0.488 mm, so 0.416 mm is BELOW the precision of the position being compared - i.wpos reaches the mesh PS interpolated and fp32-quantised at ulp(|wpos|) while the caster's stored depth came through VSSunDepth and the rasterizer's own interpolation, two different paths to the same number agreeing only within that quantum. A bias under one ulp cannot separate them and which way each fragment lands is effectively arbitrary: isolated triangles, exactly as reported. 26422 SET THIS FLOOR TO THE WRONG QUANTITY - it replaced an unanchored 10 mm constant with 4 ulp of the D32 DEPTH encode (~1.4e-3 mm) on the argument that depth quantisation was the only precision that could matter. Wrong: the depth buffer is fp32 over a ~6 m window and is microscopic, but the WORLD POSITION feeding it is fp32 over ~7 km, three orders coarser. The floor never bound because the texel term dominated it, so the error stayed hidden until 26436 removed the term accidentally covering it. THE FLOOR IS NOW 4 * ulp(|map centre|), computed exactly with nextafterf from the fit's own coordinates - 1.95 mm here - and it SCALES with world position because the limit does: at [50000,50000] ulp is 3.9 mm and the floor becomes 15.6 mm, which is not a loosened tolerance but the true precision of the comparison there. THIS IS THE SAME fp32 LIMIT AS KH_ENGINE_VIEW_PRECISION REACHED FROM THE OTHER SIDE: that ledger is the CAMERA rounding at ulp(|C|), this is the RECEIVER POSITION rounding at ulp(|wpos|) - same magnitude, same cause, and jitCamUlpMm already publishes the number every dump, which is what its own ledger meant by "it bounds every close-range registration number in this file". FAIL DIRECTION: too large peter-pans contact shadows uniformly at normal incidence; 4 ulp is 1.95 mm against occluders measured at ~200 mm, and it is the smallest value that can be meaningful at all - below one ulp the compare is comparing noise. ACCEPTANCE: the dark triangles on directly-lit faces gone, with the grazing z-fighting, the noise and the leak all still closed; 235 restoring them. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 236 up.
//  || 26437 A REUSED DESC STRUCT IS SHARED STATE, AND 26436 PUT THE BIAS IN THE WRONG BLOCK OF IT (full ledger at KH_RD_REUSE; no new mode, 234 still reverts the whole slope-location change). FIELD ON 26436: the noise went, the leak stayed gone, and the z-fighting came back STRONGER than any build before it - and both halves of that are ONE mistake. D3D11_RASTERIZER_DESC rd is a SINGLE LOCAL REUSED FOR TWELVE STATES: each block sets the fields it cares about and creates, and everything it does not set is INHERITED from whatever ran before. 26436 aimed at "the sun rasterizer's bias" and edited a DepthBias = 0 / SlopeScaledDepthBias = 0 triple - but that triple preceded the THREE PRIMARY MESH rasterizers, not rast_sun. Both directions at once: (a) rasterizer / rasterizer_cull / rasterizer_front picked up DepthBias 16 and SlopeScaledDepthBias 3.0, and 26364's ledger is explicit that these must carry 0/0/0 - it folded the mesh bias OFF after 26363 field-confirmed the box "stopped eating objects standing in front of it" - so every visible mesh draw was biased against engine depth, which is the stronger z-fighting and is 26334/26363's artifact class returned; (b) rast_sun never received the bias at all, because a later block reset the pair to 0 before it, so the sun map lost its slope compensation in the same build the shader's slope term dropped 3.0 -> 0.35. The noise still went away because the noise was only ever the per-fragment slope term and that term did shrink - three symptoms, one misplaced edit, and the one that improved is why it looked partly right. REPAIR: the bias is set immediately before rast_sun and reset to 0/0/0 immediately after rast_sun_bf, so rast_inject and everything below are untouched; rast_sun and its back-face twin are the ONLY states that carry it. THE STANDING CHECK, as cheap as the seven-variant one: walk the rasterizer block, carry the rd fields forward statement by statement, and print the resolved bias/slope/clamp at EVERY CreateRasterizerState call - twelve lines, and it makes inheritance VISIBLE instead of implied. Verified at this build: primaries 0/0/0, _b twins -32/-0.25/KH_BIAS_CLAMP, _ub twins -32/-0.25/0, rast_sun and rast_sun_bf 16/3.0/0, rast_inject 0/0/0. THE GENERAL FORM, worth more than the instance: any struct filled incrementally and consumed repeatedly is shared mutable state, and an edit "at the X block" is only correct if X is where the consumer reads - matching a field assignment is not matching the block, the same error shape as 26434's khStenP one build earlier. ACCEPTANCE: noise and leak still gone, z-fighting back to at least the 26435 state and expected better (the sun map now has its per-triangle slope compensation for the first time). FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 235 up.
//  || 26436 THE SLOPE BIAS BELONGS IN THE RASTERIZER, AND PUTTING IT ANYWHERE ELSE IS WHY THIS WAS NOISY (full ledger at KH_RASTER_SLOPE_BIAS; 234 reverts). FIELD ON 26435: the straddle guard did not close the noise. THE OPERATOR ASKED THE RIGHT QUESTION - why do other games not have this - and the answer is that they do not compute the slope bias in the COMPARE: every shadow-map implementation of consequence sets SlopeScaledDepthBias on the SHADOW PASS rasterizer and lets the hardware apply it PER PRIMITIVE, from the triangle actually being rasterized. rast_sun has carried DepthBias 0 and SlopeScaledDepthBias 0 since it was written, and every bit of slope compensation was pushed downstream into the per-fragment compare. THAT IS THE NOISE, MECHANICALLY: a per-fragment bias is computed from a NORMAL - interpolated (26431) or derivative-estimated (26432) - and scaled by tan, which is unbounded and violently sensitive near grazing; where the normal varies quickly, which is what tight geometry MEANS, the bias varies with it, and a binary compare against a rapidly varying threshold IS speckle. 26435's guard removed one source of that variation (straddling quads) and could not remove the rest, because the rest is not an error - it is the interpolated normal correctly varying across a curved strap. A PER-TRIANGLE BIAS HAS NO FRAGMENT-TO-FRAGMENT VARIANCE AT ALL: that is the whole property and why the standard technique does not noise - constant across a face by construction, whatever its magnitude, so it can be as large as grazing demands without ever making two neighbouring fragments disagree. THE UNITS ARE THE QUANTITY THIS FILE HAS BEEN COMPUTING BY HAND ALL ALONG: hardware applies SlopeScaledDepthBias * maxDepthSlope with maxDepthSlope in depth per PIXEL, and a pixel of this ortho map is a TEXEL, so 3.0f is "three texels of this triangle's own ramp" - the same figure 26431 derived for the 3x3 kernel's 2.12-texel worst tap, except the hardware measures it off the GEOMETRY rather than off a normal, which retires the shading-vs-geometric gap that 26425, 26430, 26432 and 26435 each hit from a different direction. DepthBiasClamp STAYS 0 DELIBERATELY: the clamp exists to stop a per-fragment bias blowing up, there is nothing to blow up unevenly per triangle, and clamping would reintroduce exactly the 26431 shortfall. The shader keeps a small residual (0.35 texels, was 3.0) for what the raster bias cannot see - the 3x3 kernel reaches 2.12 texels beyond the fragment and the hardware biases the caster, not the kernel - small enough that its variation is below the compare's resolution. WHAT THIS RETIRES IF IT HOLDS: 26432's facet normal and 26435's straddle guard both exist to feed a per-fragment slope term now at 0.35 of its former weight; if the field confirms, they are candidates for DELETION rather than tuning, and 233 already stands them down. NOTE 233 IS RENUMBERED to lighting0.y 19 to make room for 234 at 18 - same meaning, no recycling. ACCEPTANCE: the tight-geometry speckle gone at mode 0 with the grazing z-fighting still closed and the 26430 leak fix holding; 234 restoring both by putting the slope back in the shader and zeroing the raster bias. FAIL DIRECTION: too much raster bias peter-pans contact shadows off thin geometry uniformly (not noisily) - lower the 3.0f, do not clamp it. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 235 up.
//  || 26435 THE FACET NORMAL IS ONLY A NORMAL WHEN THE QUAD LIES ON ONE SURFACE (full ledger at KH_FACET_STRADDLE; 233 still reverts the facet slope entirely). FIELD, and the attribution is clean because the operator A/B'd the builds separately: 26431 (uncapped slope) was "almost imperceptible, still kind of there" with NO noise; 26432 (facet normal) made the z-fighting "much better" and introduced NOISE in very tight geometry - a harness strap crossing under another - while leaving open areas clean. One build, one new symptom, one suspect. MECHANISM: khFacetN is cross(ddx(wpos), ddy(wpos)), which is the true rasterized plane ONLY while the 2x2 quad lies on one surface; where it straddles a fold, a silhouette or two straps a millimetre apart, the derivatives span different geometry and the cross product is not any surface's normal - it can point anywhere, and near-grazing is a large share of anywhere. AND 26432's OWN RULE GUARANTEED THE WORST CASE: it took whichever normal grazes HARDER, argued as one-directional because the bias may only grow. That holds for two VALID normals; against a garbage one it is exactly backwards - it SELECTS the garbage whenever the garbage is more grazing, i.e. most of the time, and maximises the bias precisely where geometry is tightest and the real occluder is millimetres away. The verdict flips per QUAD, which is why it reads as 2x2 speckle rather than a smooth leak. THE GUARD IS THE ONE FACT THAT SEPARATES THE CASES: a smoothed surface's facet cannot be far from its interpolated normal - that is what smoothing MEANS, and authoring tools cap the smoothing angle well under 60 deg - while a straddling quad has no such bound. The facet is accepted only while dot(facet, interpolated) > 0.5; past that the quad spans a discontinuity and the vertex normal is the only honest answer. A constant, but anchored to the AUTHORING CONVENTION rather than to this artifact: it does not sit between two measured populations and is not tuned to one. FAIL DIRECTION BOTH WAYS so the next reading is unambiguous - NOISE SURVIVING at mode 0 means the guard is too loose or the facet is not the author, and 233 settles it in one screenshot (noise gone under 233 keeps the facet convicted and the guard wants tightening; noise present under 233 exonerates 26432 outright and the author is 26431's uncapped slope, whose knob is the ceiling); Z-FIGHTING RETURNING on smooth grazing surfaces means the guard is too tight and rejecting valid facets, so raise toward 0.7. Seven-variant declaration check re-run clean. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 234 up.
//  || 26434 COMPILE FIX: THE TWIN-EDIT RULE UNDERCOUNTS THE VARIANTS (full ledger at KH_ARB_VARIANT_EDIT; mode 0 behaviour is 26432's, unchanged). 26432 declared khFacetN/khBiasN "beside khStenP" and matched that line in both mesh shaders - two occurrences, twin edit satisfied. But PSComposite declares khStenP TWICE, in the two branches of #if KH_ARB_DEPTH, so the insert landed only in the #else while the consumer sits below the #endif: composite PS(arb) failed X3004 undeclared identifier 'khBiasN' and X3013 'SunShadowFactorSelf': no matching 2 parameter function, taking the whole composite chain with it. THE RULE WAS NEVER "TWO SHADERS" - the unit that must be checked is the VARIANT: static/composite x textured/plain x arb/non-arb x user-mat is seven live compiles off one source, and a #if inside either mesh shader multiplies the twins rather than pairing them. 26319 wrote the ordering half of this law (a callee's BODY must precede its call in every ASSEMBLY); this is the DECLARATION half - A LOCAL MUST BE DECLARED ON EVERY PREPROCESSOR PATH THAT REACHES ITS USE, and matching an anchor line twice proves nothing when the anchor is itself inside a conditional. FIX: the block moves AFTER the #endif so both arms reach it. Contrast khShN (26429), correct as written for the opposite reason - declared inside BOTH arms of its #if KH_TEXTURED. Declared once outside, or once in every arm; never once in one arm. AND IT WAS CAUGHT BY 26422: the operator saw entry point, HRESULT and fxc's own two errors with file and line because kh_ensure_ok routes the composite failure through the dedupe instead of dropping it at an .empty() test - under 26421 this would have presented as the 26319 no-spawn, gatePassed 0 and compositeSkips climbing with no message anywhere. The reporting fix paid for itself. STANDING CHECK, and it is cheap: preprocess the concatenated HLSL once per define set and assert every local is declared before its first use in each - seven variants, comments stripped,