//  || 26433 THE STANDING-START STENCIL DRAG: LOCATED, AND THE ESCAPE BUILT FOR IT CANNOT REACH IT (full ledger at KH_TRN_FLOOR_RESIDUAL; NO BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, no number spent). FIELD (dump2, 5912 injects): the operator caught ONE stencil-shadow drag on abrupt motion. svLiveTrnBound 9 session-wide, TWO in the 512-row window, and both are one event to four decimals - ser 5677 and 5777, camStepM[k-1] and [k] BOTH EXACTLY 0.0000 with a real 1.2206 / 1.2395 m move at k+1, bnd 0.3000 (THE FLOOR BRANCH), primeCamDxM[k+1] == seamTrnDxM exactly. The footprint rode a full camera step behind for one frame: the drag, with complete per-frame coverage. THIS IS THE CLASS 26402/26404/26405 EACH DELIBERATELY LEFT - 26405 lowered the still line 0.02 -> 0.005 and closed the walking-start population (camStepM[k] 0.0174-0.0190), but here camStepM[k] is EXACTLY 0.0000, a true dead stop, so it is the STILL branch where the 0.30 m floor is the only separator from the proven foreign class (ser 3642, 2.593 m off a stationary latch). MAGNITUDE CANNOT SEPARATE THEM: legitimate 1.22-1.24 m off a dead stop against foreign 2.593 m off a dead stop is 2.1x with the floor under both, and rule 1.2 forbids a bar in that gap - the population-edge shape 26252 and 26405 were each written about. THE ESCAPE CANNOT HELP BY CONSTRUCTION AND ITS NULL IS DECISIVE, NOT VOID: it fires only when a refusal finds g_khlt_ref_ok already set, i.e. on a SECOND CONSECUTIVE refusal, and a standing-start jump produces exactly ONE (the next frame's reference is the real 1.22 m step, the bound widens to 4x it, the take passes and the else branch clears the flag). svLiveTrnRebase reads 0 across 5912 injects WITH 9 refusals - 26306's "a real relocation confirms itself in one publication" can never see a ONE-FRAME relocation, because confirmation is retrospective and the frame is already drawn. THE SAME-FRAME WITNESS EXISTS AND IS MEASURED: on BOTH refusal rows the fetch carries seamProjSrc 6 (the identity-keyed bound-CB census pair) with censusMatch 4, and seamLiveNear == boundPairNear to five figures (3.92604 vs 3.9255; 0.223122 vs 0.22312). A foreign publication carries ANOTHER WINDOW'S projection - 26295's conviction, 26302's whole corridor - so a fetch whose own projection matches the census pair is provably from our window whatever its camera magnitude. That is a WITNESS GATE and not a threshold: it spends no constant and still refuses ser-3642, whose pair fails it. WHY IT IS NOT FOLDED: the projection arbitration producing that witness runs AFTER this take in the seam's ordering (~450 lines below), so the value is not in scope at the bound; using it means moving the pair resolution ahead of the translation take, reordering the most heavily-ledgered path in this file with 26331's wrong-space landing as the standing risk. A CAMPAIGN, NOT A PATCH, and n = 2 does not buy it. WHAT WOULD, per 26406: publish the pair-agreement verdict AT THE BOUND as a per-refusal lane, run a session of deliberate dead-stop starts, and read whether it separates the classes at n in the dozens - if it does the reorder is justified with acceptance svLiveTrnBound collapsing to foreign-projection rows only; if it does not, the floor is the final form and the one-frame drag on a dead-stop jump is the accepted cost, which at 2 events in 512 frames of provoked motion it may well be. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 234 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26432 THE BIAS SLOPE TAKES THE FACET, NOT THE VERTEX NORMAL - THE LAST PRINCIPLED LEVER, AND THE FLOOR IS NAMED (full ledger at KH_FACET_BIAS_NORMAL; 233 reverts). FIELD ON 26431: grazing z-fighting from obvious to ALMOST IMPERCEPTIBLE, self-shadowing correct, leak closed. THE NAMED RESIDUAL: the bias slope came from ndl on the INTERPOLATED VERTEX NORMAL while the sun map holds RASTERIZED FACETS; on a smoothed FBX those differ by degrees and the bias is proportional to tan, which near grazing is brutally sensitive - between ndl 0.06 and 0.05 the required bias moves 20 percent - so wherever the interpolated normal grazes LESS than the facet it sits on, the bias comes out short and those texels flicker. Same shading-vs-geometric gap that 26425's gradient died of and 26430's veto hid behind: third and last appearance. THE FIX takes the facet normal from the screen-space derivative of world position (which IS the rasterized plane, by construction) and uses whichever normal grazes the sun HARDER; since bias grows with tan that is ONE-DIRECTIONAL - it can only increase the bias and cannot un-shadow anything 26430 now shadows - with degenerate zero-area quads falling back to the vertex normal instead of producing a NaN. TAKEN IN STRAIGHT-LINE FLOW beside khStenP, which already does exactly this for the stencil walk, because 26277's rule stands: ddx/ddy are UNDEFINED inside the divergent N.L gate. AND NOW THE FLOOR, because past this point bias is the wrong lever: one map texel is 0.48975 mm at this fit and spans 0.48975/ndl ACROSS the receiving surface - 0.98 mm at ndl 0.5, 3.95 mm at 0.124, 8.16 mm at 0.06, 24.49 mm at 0.02 - so at grazing the shadow boundary is quantised at CENTIMETRE scale on the surface whatever the compare does. That is the map's RESOLUTION, not a bias failure, and no bias, kernel or normal can move it. The only levers past 26432 are KH_SUN_DEPTH_SIZE (already 4096) and the ortho fit (already tightened at 26422 part 2, which bought 3.3x alone); a future campaign should start THERE and price it first - doubling the map is 4x the memory for 2x the boundary precision, which at ndl 0.02 takes 24 mm to 12 mm and is still centimetres. ACCEPTANCE: the last of the grazing flicker gone or provably at the resolution floor, with the 26430 leak fix and all cast/cascade behaviour holding; 233 restoring the vertex-normal slope. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 234 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26431 THE GRAZING Z-FIGHTING IS GUARANTEED BY A CEILING THAT NO LONGER HAS A SUBJECT (full ledger at KH_SLOPE_CEILING; 232 restores the ceiling). FIELD ON 26430: self-shadowing correct on mode 0, leak closed, grazing z-fighting remaining. THE BIAS IS SHORT AND BY HOW MUCH IS COMPUTABLE: the 3x3 kernel's worst tap sits 2.12 texels away, so the receiver's own plane climbs 2.12 * tan * texelWorld across it, while the bias supplied was 3 * min(tan, 8) * texelWorld. At this fit (sunMapHalfDiag 0.983351, texelWorld 0.48975 mm) the needed ramp is 8.31 mm at ndl 0.124 against 11.75 mm of bias (ok), then 12.94 at 0.080, 17.27 at 0.060, 25.94 at 0.040 and 51.90 at 0.020 - all against the same frozen 11.75. BELOW ndl ~0.10 THE BIAS IS MATHEMATICALLY INCAPABLE of clearing the ramp, so some taps read occluded and their neighbours do not: the z-fighting is guaranteed, not incidental, and no kernel, gradient or map change could have removed it while the ceiling stood - which is a fair summary of 26423 through 26428. THE CEILING IS A LEFTOVER FROM A DIFFERENT QUANTITY: it is 26400's KH_SELF_SLOPE_MAX, whose ledger justifies it honestly for what it capped AT THE TIME - an offset that MOVED THE SAMPLE POINT along the normal, which unbounded walks off the geometry through a silhouette or out of a crevice. 26422 part 3 removed the slope scaling from that lateral half and 26425 removed the offsets entirely; what survives is a PURE DEPTH BIAS, and the two have OPPOSITE cost curves. The ceiling outlived its subject by six builds. WHY UNCAPPING IS SAFE, and this is the part to keep: a pure depth bias slope-scaled by tan produces a SURFACE-SPACE leak of bias/tan = 3 texels, CONSTANT at every incidence - growing the depth bias without bound does not grow the leak along the surface at all, it stays 3 * 0.48975 = 1.47 mm whether tan is 1.7 or 50. That is the whole reason slope-scaled depth bias is the standard form and exactly what the lateral offset could never claim. THE ONE TRADE, STATED: the bias is still a depth and can in principle out-reach a genuine occluder; ndl is floored at 0.02 so tan tops out near 50 and the bias near 73 mm at this fit, against the leak occluders visual 19 measured at roughly 200 mm - 2.7x margin at the very worst incidence and far more everywhere else. IF THE 26430 LEAK EVER RETURNS AT EXTREME GRAZING, THE CEILING IS THE KNOB and it now has a stated meaning (maximum depth bias, in texels of ramp); do not reach for the kernel or the gradient again. Visual 19's bias tracks the term's so ORANGE/GREEN keeps meaning what it says - its useful divergence is STRUCTURAL (one tap, no N.L veto), not this constant. ACCEPTANCE: grazing z-fighting gone at mode 0 with the 26430 leak fix holding, shadowed regions and casts unchanged; 232 restoring the flicker. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 233 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26430 THE SAME BUG ONE LEVEL DEEPER, MEASURED BY VISUAL 7 (full ledger at KH_GEOMETRIC_NL_VETO; 231 restores the veto). Visual 7 paints (color.a, SolidMask, smf), so BLUE IS smf: yellow = smf 0 = shadowed, white = smf 1 = unshadowed. The capture shows the leaking patch WHITE surrounded by yellow - the term genuinely returns UNSHADOWED - while visual 19 paints the SAME fragment GREEN, i.e. the sun map holds an occluder well clear of the bias. Two computations that are supposed to be identical, disagreeing on one pixel, and one screenshot found it. THE DIFFERENCE IS ONE LINE: SunShadowOcclusionSelf opened with `if (ndl <= 0.0f) return 0.0f;` (26425) taken from the GEOMETRIC normal, while SunSelfProbe has no such veto - it computes ndl only to size the bias. So on a facet whose geometry faces away from the sun the term returns zero occlusion immediately, smf stays 1.0, and the textured path lights the fragment through khtxN which the normal map has turned sun-facing: fully lit, no shadow term, occluder present in the map and never consulted. AND IT EXPLAINS 230 READING IDENTICAL TO MODE 0, which looked like a dead end and was the clue: under the 26429 gate the block runs and this veto kills the lookup, under 230's geometric gate the block never runs at all, and BOTH ROUTES LEAVE smf AT 1.0 - a null that was not void, exactly like 229 before it. 26429 was necessary (the block must run for the cascade and stencil terms too) but not sufficient alone, and that is recorded rather than left looking like a failed build. WHY THE VETO IS WRONG AND NOT MERELY MIS-SCOPED: a shadow-map lookup does not need N.L for anything - ndl enters this function ONLY as the slope input to the bias and is already floored at 0.02 inside the tangent. It was added at 26425 as a cheap guard for "a face with no direct term has nothing to attenuate", which is the GATE's job and which the gate now does correctly against the normal that actually lights the fragment; doing it twice against two different normals is what produced the hole. WHAT CHANGES: a back-facing facet whose mapped normal faces the sun now gets its lookup and the map answers what it always held - the receiver sits behind the front face stored at that texel, so it reads OCCLUDED. Correct, and one-directional: this can only remove light, never add it. Fragments unlit under BOTH normals are still culled by the gate. ACCEPTANCE: the white hole in visual 7 fills in with yellow, the leak closes on screen, shadowed regions and casts unchanged; 231 restoring the hole. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 232 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26429 THE SELF-SHADOW TERM WAS NEVER RUNNING ON THE FRAGMENTS THAT LEAK (full ledger at KH_SHADING_NORMAL_GATE; 230 reverts). The whole receive block in both mesh shaders sat behind `dot(i.nrm, lighting1.xyz) > 0.01f` - the GEOMETRIC normal - while the textured path shades through KhApplyPBR/KhUserShade with khtxN, the NORMAL-MAPPED normal, and computes its own ndl from that. A fragment whose GEOMETRY grazes the sun therefore skipped the block outright: no cascade receive, no self-shadow, no stencil multiply, smf left at 1.0, and then lit through a mapped normal that fabric and webbing routinely perturb well past the sun line. A fully lit fragment with no shadow term applied at all. ONE MECHANISM, BOTH SYMPTOMS: it fires only where the geometric N.L straddles 0.01, which is exactly the "grazing parts" reported every round; and the 0.01 test is BINARY, so neighbouring fragments either side of it flip between "block ran" and "block did not" per pixel - the stipple was NEVER acne, no bias could ever have damped it, and six builds of bias work demonstrated precisely that. AND IT IS WHY EVERY COMPARE-SIDE BUILD FAILED: 26422 (bias floor), 26423/26424 (the escape), 26425 (receiver-plane depth bias), 26426 (back-face map) and 26428 (gradient off) all modified SunShadowOcclusionSelf, A FUNCTION NOT REACHED on the leaking fragments. MODE 229 READING IDENTICAL TO MODE 0 IS THAT FACT IN WRITING, and it is what forced this read - a null that was not void but decisive. VISUAL 19 NAMED IT WITHOUT NAMING IT: it paints the leaked parts and the shadowed parts BOTH GREEN, so the sun map holds a genuine occluder in front of both; the map was right the whole time and the consumer was never asked. The probe runs OUTSIDE this gate (dispatched from the debug branch at the top of the shader), which is the SECOND time its divergence from the shading path is what made it useful - keep it divergent. THE FIX gates on the normal that actually lights the fragment: khtxN where the textured path provides one, i.nrm otherwise. THE SELF-SHADOW CALL KEEPS THE GEOMETRIC NORMAL - SunShadowFactorSelf(i.wpos, i.nrm) is unchanged and must stay so, because its bias is sized against the RASTERIZED facets the sun map holds, not against the shading normal. Gate on what is lit; bias against what was drawn; conflating the two is 26425's error from the other direction. COST, small and one-directional: fragments whose geometry faces away but whose mapped normal faces the sun now RECEIVE shadow where they were unconditionally lit, which can only remove light that should never have been there; the gate still culls everything with no direct term under either normal, so its optimisation survives. TWIN EDIT, both PSMain and PSComposite. ACCEPTANCE: the grazing light bleed and the grazing flicker gone TOGETHER at mode 0, shadowed regions and casts unchanged, 230 restoring both. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 231 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26428 THE GRADIENT WAS THE LEAK, AND THE PROBE CAUGHT IT BY BEING DIFFERENT FROM THE TERM (full ledger at KH_SELF_RPDB_FALSIFIED; 229 restores the gradient). FIELD ON VISUAL 19, all three region classes in one screenshot: the LEAKED (lit) parts paint GREEN, the SHADOWED parts paint GREEN, and the Z-FIGHTING parts alternate ORANGE/BLUE - gap oscillating either side of zero. (Orange reads yellow on screen: these returns go through the HDR composite and the tonemap, which is 26410's own warning and the palette should have accounted for it.) THE DECISIVE FACT IS THAT THE PROBE CANNOT TELL THE LEAKED PARTS FROM THE SHADOWED ONES - both GREEN - so the sun map contains a real occluder in front of BOTH and the entire capture side (caster set, ortho fit, coverage, the map's contents) is CORRECT. That eliminates half the pipeline in one read, which five builds of compare-side guessing never managed. WHAT SEPARATES THEM IS THE ONE THING THE PROBE DOES DIFFERENTLY: it compares a single centre tap with the slope-scaled bias and NO gradient, while the term ran a 3x3 with the 26425 receiver-plane gradient. On the leaked parts - the grazing ones - that gradient is enormous (k = texelWorld * invD / ndl, so at ndl 0.05 a one-texel offset shifts the predicted depth twenty times what it does at 45 degrees); taps disagree wildly, several predict a depth far below the stored value and read UNOCCLUDED, and the nine-tap average collapses. The term reported lit on a fragment its own centre tap calls solidly occluded. THE GRADIENT WAS NOT CORRECTING THE COMPARE, IT WAS DILUTING IT. WHY THE ARITHMETIC WAS RIGHT AND THE RESULT STILL WRONG - 26426 named this and failed to act on it: the gradient is built from the INTERPOLATED SHADING NORMAL while the map holds RASTERIZED FACETS, on a smoothed FBX those differ by degrees, and 1/ndl multiplies that error without bound. A receiver-plane bias is only as good as the plane it is handed, and a shading normal is not that plane. THE TERM NOW IS THE PROBE'S OWN ARITHMETIC: 3x3 point kernel, slope-scaled bias, no gradient - not a guess but the exact computation the operator just read off the screen, whose verdict on every visible region is already known: leaked GREEN -> occluded -> THE LEAK CLOSES; shadowed GREEN -> unchanged; z-fighting ORANGE/BLUE -> gap <= bias -> not occluded, uniformly and stably LIT -> THE FLICKER CLOSES. COST, stated: those grazing self-compare patches shade lit rather than shadowed - they were lit at the 26421 baseline too, so nothing regresses, and they are now lit WITHOUT flickering. THE LESSON IS WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX: the probe was built as an instrument and became the CONTROL because it re-implemented the same question a DIFFERENT way; five compare-side builds moved together with the thing they were measuring and could never disagree with it. 1.16 says the instrument is part of the hypothesis - this is the corollary, that an instrument DUPLICATING the implementation can only ever confirm it. VISUAL 19 MUST NOT BE "CORRECTED" TO MATCH THE TERM: that divergence is the whole of its value. ACCEPTANCE: leak gone, flicker gone, shadowed regions unchanged, casts and cascades untouched; 229 restoring both symptoms together. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 230 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26427 THE INSTRUMENT, AT LAST - DEBUG VISUAL 19 (setRenderDebug 228; full ledger at KH_SUN_MAP_PROBE). PURE GAUGE, mode 0 byte-identical. FIELD ON 26426: mode 227 brought the z-fighting flicker back and did NOTHING for the light bleed. AND THE ARM WAS DEFECTIVE - 227 changed the rasterizer AND dropped the bias, so its z-fighting result cannot be attributed to either half; that is a two-thing A/B in a file whose rules say one, and it is mine. What it does say, because both halves point the same way, is that back faces did not separate the receiver from the stored surface, and THE LIKELY READING IS ZERO-THICKNESS SHEETS: cull-front on a sheet keeps a back face at the SAME position as the front, the receiver still compares against itself, and the interval is still empty. If that holds, no culling scheme fixes this and the compare side is closed for good. STOP. FIVE BUILDS HAVE NOW CHANGED THE COMPARE - 26422 the bias floor, 26423/26424 the escape, 26425 receiver-plane depth bias, 26426 the back-face map - and the verdict has been worse or unchanged every time, because NOTHING IN THIS FILE REPORTS WHAT THE SUN MAP CONTAINS at the fragment being shaded. Every one of those rounds reasoned about a value nobody had looked at. That is 26406's finding on the cascade axis repeated verbatim here, and it was settled there by a shader visual in ONE round after eight builds of inference. WHAT IT PAINTS at the fragment's own unbiased texel: GRAY no map; MAGENTA the fragment is outside the map (uv or z rejected) = COVERAGE fault, the fit is wrong and not the compare; RED the texel holds the CLEAR value, no caster was ever rendered there so nothing can shadow this fragment however the compare is written = CAPTURE side; BLUE gap <= 0, the map holds nothing in front of this fragment - on a region that ought to be shadowed by other geometry this is decisive, the occluder is simply not in the map; ORANGE 0 < gap <= bias, there IS a real occluder and THE BIAS IS EATING IT = peter-panning in writing, leak is bias-side and bounded; GREEN gap > bias, a genuine occluder the bias does not eat, brightness = how far past the bias, saturating at 10x. PRE-REGISTERED READING, branches naming different subsystems with no overlap: leak ORANGE = bias-side, attack the empty interval (two-pass map, or a thickness-aware bias); leak BLUE or RED = the occluding geometry is not in the map at that texel and NO compare-side change can ever fix it, next stop is render_sun_depth itself (what it draws, the caster set, the ortho fit); leak MAGENTA = coverage, and 26422 part 2's lateral extent split is the first suspect; leak GREEN = the term reports SHADOWED while the screen shows lit, so the loss is DOWNSTREAM in the smf chain or the lighting path and this function is exonerated outright. Read the stipple the same way: alternating ORANGE/GREEN on one surface is the self-compare aliasing; alternating RED/GREEN is the map itself being holed. DO NOT FOLD ANYTHING ON THIS AXIS UNTIL THIS HAS BEEN READ. Mode 0 keeps 26426's restored slope-scaled bias (acne suppressed, leak present) so the operator is not left worse than the 26421 baseline while the probe is run. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 229 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26426 THE INTERVAL IS EMPTY BECAUSE THE MAP STORES OUR OWN FRONT FACES (full ledger at KH_SUN_BACKFACE; 227 arms the back-face map). FIELD ON 26425: z-fighting HARDER and the leak back at baseline, and mode 183 removes the shadows wholesale so it does not isolate. FOUR ROUNDS ON ONE FUNCTION WITH NO INSTRUMENT IS WHAT 26404 AND 26406 FORBID, and this entry is where that stops. WHAT THE ROUNDS DID ESTABLISH: (1) the 10 mm compare-bias floor 26422 removed as unanchored was LOAD-BEARING - it was also the only thing suppressing grazing acne, so removing it traded the crevice leak for stipple and the whole 26422-26425 symptom history is that trade; it should have been predicted from the same empty-interval argument that was already written down. (2) Mode 226 returned LIT AND STIPPLED TOGETHER, which is one aliasing signature and not two faults. (3) RPDB is CORRECT ARITHMETIC - row_major is declared on sunVP, the basis comes out of its columns as documented, the gradient is the receiver plane's true slope - and it STILL did not fix it, which eliminates the slope term as sole author and points at the SHADING-vs-GEOMETRIC normal gap: the gradient is built from the interpolated vertex normal while the map holds rasterized facets, and on a smoothed FBX those differ by degrees. THE ROOT IS UPSTREAM OF THE COMPARE. rast_sun is CullNone, so the map stores the NEAREST surface along the sun ray, which for any lit receiver IS THAT RECEIVER. Every version of this term since 26400 has been asking a surface not to shadow itself; the bias that clears its own ramp at grazing is unbounded while the bias that does not walk out of thin geometry is millimetres, so THE INTERVAL IS EMPTY BY CONSTRUCTION and no compare, gradient or fade can widen it. 227 STORES THE BACK FACES (CullFront): the receiver's front face is then never the stored surface, the stored depth is the far side of the object centimetres away, and the compare needs essentially no bias, cannot acne, cannot peter-pan, and reads a genuine occluder correctly at any incidence. One rasterizer state, created non-fatally with a fall-through to rast_sun so a creation failure makes the mode inert rather than a null bind. IT IS AN ARM AND NOT A DEFAULT because rast_sun's own ledger keeps CullNone so an OPEN mesh still casts a closed-surface shadow, and under 227 a single-sided panel has no back face and CASTS NOTHING - whether that matters depends on how the operator's models are built, which is a field question. MODE 0 MEANWHILE RETREATS TO KNOWN-GOOD: the slope-scaled compare bias is restored at 3 texels of ramp, reproducing the 26421 anti-acne magnitude at the harsh end (~11.8 mm at tan 8 against the old flat 10 mm floor) while spending ~1.5 mm at 45 deg where the floor spent the full 10 - so mode 0 is no worse than the baseline the operator knows, with the leak present and the stipple gone. THE READ, one screenshot per arm: 227 fixing BOTH with casts intact = fold it, drop the bias to quantisation and delete the 26422-26425 compare-side machinery with it; 227 fixing them while parts STOP CASTING = the models are open and the fix is a two-pass map (back faces for self, CullNone for cast), which is a build not a patch; 227 changing NEITHER = the artifact is not the self-compare, the axis is wrong, and the next thing built is a debug visual painting the sun map at the fragment's own uv the way visual 18 settled the cascade. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 228 up; 216/225/226 are DELISTED-not-free.
//  || 26425 THE SELF-SHADOW COMPARE IS MADE EXACT AND THE WHOLE OFFSET/FADE/ESCAPE APPARATUS RETIRES WITH IT (full ledger at KH_SELF_RPDB; 183 is the A/B, and 216/225/226 are DELISTED with the term they armed while 224 keeps only its CPU half). FIELD ON 26424: the grazing z-fighting persisted at mode 0, and mode 226 - cut and fade off, the split this build shipped to answer exactly this - came back LIT AND STIPPLED AT ONCE. That pair is not two faults and it is not an escape that needs widening: it is one aliasing signature, the plane's depth sweeping past the stored value inside a single texel so neighbouring fragments disagree. dump2 confirms the geometry is stable and small (sunLocalCount 1, sunMapHalfDiag 0.983351 identical to dump1, sunMapValid 1, mature, sun elevation 23.4 deg from sunDirDerivedY 0.397) - nothing upstream is wrong, so the pre-registered 'stays LIT means the fault is upstream' branch of 226 is answered NO and the fault is the compare itself. WHY NO BIAS COULD HAVE WORKED: every version from 26400 to 26424 compared ONE depth against a neighbourhood of stored depths and needed a bias big enough to clear the receiver's own ramp across a texel yet small enough not to leave the geometry. The ramp is texelWorld * tan(theta) and tan is UNBOUNDED, so at grazing that interval is EMPTY - clearing it at ndl 0.05 costs centimetres and peter-pans through thin features, not clearing it leaves the compare flipping per texel. 26400 resolved the contradiction by fading the whole term out below ndl 0.124 (absent beats wrong), and every symptom since has been that fade or an attempt to work around it. THE FIX STOPS COMPARING A CONSTANT. The receiver is a plane and in an orthographic map its depth over the texel grid is exactly linear - dz/dx = n_sun.x/ndl, dz/dy = n_sun.y/ndl, all of it recoverable from sunVP (column 0 is r3/R, column 1 is u3/R, column 2 is f/D) - so each tap compares against the plane's OWN predicted depth at that texel's centre. The residual drops from first order in the slope to second order in the curvature and stops growing as ndl falls. THAT DELETES THE APPARATUS RATHER THAN TUNING IT: the sun-ward and lateral offsets go (nothing is displaced, so nothing can walk out of a crevice - the thin-slot leak is fixed by construction rather than by shrinking a bias), the grazing cut and fade go (the compare is valid at grazing, so there is nothing to fade away from), and the distant-occluder escape goes (it existed only to let the fade be skipped). ndl is floored at 0.02 for the gradient - past that the plane is edge-on, the prediction is worthless and the surface takes no direct light, so nothing visible rides on it. CHEAPER INCIDENTALLY: nine point Loads against twenty. KNOWN AND ACCEPTED, the standard cost of this method: a silhouette texel holds a different surface's depth, disagrees with the plane prediction and shades occluded, so silhouettes carry a ~1 texel dark rim - defensible (that texel really is occluded from the sun) and far smaller than a whole-face light leak. ACCEPTANCE: thin-crevice leak, grazing leak and grazing z-fighting all gone together at mode 0, cast shadows and cascade receive unchanged, 183 removing the self term entirely. IF ANYTHING SURVIVES, 183 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND IT IS A REAL ONE: dying under 183 keeps it in this term, SURVIVING 183 puts it outside the sun-map self-shadow and no further work belongs here. FREE NUMBERS: 223, 225, 226 and 227 up - but 225/226 are DELISTED-not-free and must not be recycled (1.18); truly free are 223 and 227 up.
//  || 26424 THE 26423 ESCAPE USED THE CLAMPED SLOPE AS ITS RAMP BOUND, AND THAT IS WHAT STIPPLED (full ledger at KH_SELF_GRAZING_ESCAPE; 225 = escape off, 226 = cut and fade off as the residual split). FIELD ON 26423: the crevice and grazing leaks both improved and grazing faces of the same mesh began Z-FIGHTING - the exact failure the fade exists to prevent, appearing where the escape had switched the fade off. dump1 settles it by arithmetic with no further capture: sunMapHalfDiag 0.983351 gives R_lat 1.0030, D 5.9667 and texelWorld 0.48975 mm, so the escape line at the clamped khss_k of 8 is 4 * 0.48975 * 8 = 15.67 mm, while the surface's OWN depth ramp over the PCF's ~2.5 texel footprint reads 12.18 mm at ndl 0.10, 20.37 mm at ndl 0.06 and 40.79 mm at ndl 0.03. Below ndl ~0.08 the ramp crosses the line, the escape fires on SELF-comparison, the fade is skipped and the compare flips per texel. THE ERROR IS MINE AND IT IS A CATEGORY ERROR: khss_k is clamped to 8 because it sizes the OFFSET (26400's KH_SELF_SLOPE_MAX, correctly), whereas the RAMP it was being compared against has no ceiling - it is texelWorld * tan(theta) and tan grows without bound as ndl -> 0 - so reusing one quantity for the other made the line too small in precisely the regime the grazing machinery exists for. THE UNCLAMPED tan IS THE EXACT RAMP, NOT A LOOSER BOUND: the receiver plane's depth gradient over the ortho map is |n_sun.xy|/|n_sun.z| = sqrt(1-ndl^2)/ndl per texel, which IS tan(theta), so the receiver-plane-gradient derivation and the analytic form agree and no safety factor is being spent. It self-corrects at both ends - 2.12 mm at ndl 0.5 so genuine occluders still escape trivially, growing past any plausible gap at true grazing so the fade resumes exactly where it was always meant to. The 4x multiplier is the PCF footprint and not a knob: five bilinear taps spread over ~2.5 texels, so the ramp actually sampled is 2.5x the per-texel figure and 4x clears it with 1.6x spare. MODE 226 IS THE RESIDUAL SPLIT and costs no build: cut and fade off unconditionally, so a lit region that goes DARK under it is still being eaten by the grazing terms and the escape wants widening, while one that stays LIT is not being found by the sun-depth map at all and the fault is upstream of the receive entirely. Expect stipple while 226 is armed - that is what the fade is for, and it is why 226 is a diagnostic and not a candidate. ACCEPTANCE: the grazing z-fighting gone at mode 0, the 26422/26423 leak results holding, 225 restoring the lit face and 226 naming whatever is left. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 227 and up (223 was never spent in this lineage - see KH_FROZEN_CAMERA_TEST).
//  || 26423 THE SECOND LEAK IS THE GRAZING FADE, NOT A BIAS (full ledger at KH_SELF_GRAZING_ESCAPE; 225 reverts, and it is a SEPARATE axis from 224 by design). FIELD ON 26422: a strap spanning the front of a vest reads SUNLIT while the entire left panel of the same mesh stands between it and the sun - a ~20 cm separation, two orders of magnitude past anything 26422 touches, so the peter-panning mechanism cannot explain it and the crevice fix correctly did not. MECHANISM: SunShadowOcclusionSelf ends with `return occ * smoothstep(khss_lo, 0.20, ndl)` over a hard `if (ndl < khss_lo) return 0`, both from 26400 and both sound on their own terms - at harsh incidence the normal offset can no longer cover a texel, the bias is capped while the span keeps growing, and the compare starts guessing, so absent beats wrong. But the fade multiplies the WHOLE occlusion term unconditionally, so a face at ndl ~0.15 loses its shadow whether the occluder is its own texel ramp or a panel 20 cm away. The strap is lit because it is GRAZING, not because anything about the compare was wrong: the PCF returns 1 and the fade multiplies it to nothing. That is a case 26400 never separated, and every previous instrument was silent on it because nothing was failing. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS FREE AND EXACT: sample the map at the receiver's OWN unbiased texel. A self-comparing surface stores its own depth there, so the gap is bounded by the ramp across one texel - texelWorld * tan(theta) - BY CONSTRUCTION. Any gap materially larger cannot have come from this surface; something else is between this point and the sun. The bound is already computed for the bias (texelWorld * khss_k) and converts to map depth through 1/D read off column 2 of sunVP; 4x of it is the escape line, a margin over a DERIVED quantity rather than a tuned threshold, and the two populations it separates are a sub-texel ramp and a 20 cm occluder. Where the escape fires the cut and the fade are skipped and the full occlusion stands; where it does not, 26400 is verbatim - so this cannot reintroduce the stipple the fade exists for, because stipple is a surface comparing against ITSELF and that is precisely the case the escape declines to take. COST: one Load on fragments reaching the self term. KNOWN AND ACCEPTED: at a silhouette the receiver's own texel may hold the nearer surface, so a ~1 texel sliver there shades fully rather than fading - that verdict is CORRECT (the texel genuinely is occluded from the sun's view) and a one-texel edge is a far smaller error than a whole grazing face losing its shadow. ACCEPTANCE: geometry that plainly occludes another part of the same mesh now shadows it at any incidence, with the 26422 crevice result and all cast/cascade behaviour unchanged; 225 restoring the lit face. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 227 and up. 223 is FREE, not reserved - a throwaway investigative branch ran a frozen-camera jitter experiment on that number, the branch is gone and nothing from it is in this build, so the number was never spent in this lineage. Its RESULT is kept at KH_FROZEN_CAMERA_TEST because it is the evidence behind the outstanding developer request, quoted with the mode label stripped so no future 223 can be misread against it.
//  || 26422 THE THIN-CREVICE LIGHT LEAK IS THE SELF-SHADOW BIAS PETER-PANNING AT MILLIMETRE SCALE (full ledger at KH_SELF_BIAS_CREVICE; 224 reverts all three parts as one switch). FIELD REPORT: a complex mesh lights, self-shadows and receives correctly, and narrow slots in it - the gap between a plate and its shell, a strap channel, a recessed seam - read SUNLIT where the slot's own lip must occlude them. Only thin features; broad concavities are correct. MECHANISM: the self term compares at wpos + selfOfs, not at the receiver, and every millimetre of that bias is a millimetre the sample climbs toward the sun and travels across the surface. Once the total exceeds the slot's depth the sample emerges above the lip into open sky and compares lit - so the shadow is not soft or shifted, it is ABSENT, and only in features smaller than the bias. That is the reported shape exactly. THREE CONTRIBUTORS, measured at a 0.5 m object (bounding radius 0.43 m, so the ortho was R = 1.43 and one texel was 0.70 mm). (1) THE COMPARE-BIAS FLOOR, the largest by far and with no anchor at all: bias_world was max(texel_world * 0.5, 0.01), a hard 10 mm floor whose stated purpose is precision - against a D32_FLOAT map whose depth quantisation is D * 6e-8, about 0.3 MICRONS on this fit, four orders of magnitude below it. And it is backwards: a fixed world floor is largest RELATIVE TO THE GEOMETRY exactly when the geometry is finest, so at a 250 m fit it never binds (texel_world * 0.5 = 61 mm) while at 0.5 m it multiplies the bias 28x to ~14 texels. Every small detailed mesh has been paying a bias sized for a quarter-kilometre fit. Replaced by the map's own depth resolution with margin - four ulp of the D32 encode over D - which is derived rather than picked and cannot bind wherever the texel term already dominates. (2) THE DEPTH GUARD BAND WAS INSIDE THE LATERAL EXTENT. R was the caster bounding radius PLUS ONE METRE and that one number sized the ortho x/y half-extent, placed the eye and set D = 2R+2. The +1 m is a depth guard; in the lateral extent it is destructive at small scales because texel size is 2R/4096, so a 0.43 m radius object had its ortho more than TRIPLED and its texel with it (0.21 -> 0.70 mm), multiplying every texel-measured bias by 3.3x. Split into R_dep (historic, drives eye/D/proj[2][2] - depth-domain behaviour byte-identical to 26421) and a lateral R at radius * 1.02, the bounding sphere containing every projection by construction. The shader derives texelWorld from column 0 of sunVP so it follows with no CB change. (3) THE SLOPE SCALE WAS ON BOTH HALVES OF selfOfs AND BELONGS ON ONE. The SUN-WARD half (lighting1.xyz * 1.25) moves the sample along the projection axis, so it changes c.z and nothing else - a pure depth bias, whose required magnitude IS texelWorld * tan(theta), so khss_k there is the definition and is unchanged. The LATERAL half (n * 1.5) is the only part that moves the sample in uv, i.e. the only part that can walk it onto different geometry; its job is to step onto a neighbouring texel and that is 1.5 texels wide by construction. Scaling it by tan(theta) escapes no ramp - the ramp is a depth quantity and this offset is lateral - it just travels up to 12 texels across the surface, through the opposite wall of any narrower slot. Slope scale retired from the lateral half only. 26399's restoration of the 1.5 MAGNITUDE stands and is preserved at full strength on every incidence; what is removed is the multiplier on top of it, which 26399 never argued for - it arrived with khss_k when the sun-ward term was introduced and the two were never separated. NET: the escape budget falls from ~25 mm to ~5 mm at the worst fade-full incidence and ~3 mm at moderate, with the depth escape untouched, so nothing that shadowed correctly can stop doing so - acne is a depth-domain failure and parts 2 and 3 move no depth. ACCEPTANCE: the lit band in thin crevices gone at mode 0 with self-shadowing, cast shadows and cascade receive all unchanged; 224 restoring the band. FAIL DIRECTION: self-acne stipple on sun-facing faces - and if it ever appears it is the TEXEL term that wants raising, never the floor. FREE NUMBERS: 223 and 227 and up (223 was never spent in this lineage - see KH_FROZEN_CAMERA_TEST).
//  || 26422 SCALE IS A MULTIPLIER (full ledger at KH_SCALE_IS_A_MULTIPLIER). addRender3D/updateRender3D 'size' is now |value| x the mesh's own native dimension on every axis, sign-free, with 0 reading as 1. The positive branch used to be an absolute EDGE LENGTH that stretched the [-0.5,0.5]^3 bake to fill a box, which is meaningful only for a cube: any mesh with unequal native dimensions was FORCED to a 1:1:1 aspect ratio, so a 0.9 x 0.15 x 1.1 m model asked for "size 2" came out a 2 m cube. Every .fbx therefore had to be spawned negative, making the negative path the real one and the positive path a trap that silently deforms geometry. Builtins author at native 1, so multiplier == metres for them and the whole historic box/sphere corpus is byte-identical; negative sizes are byte-identical on every mesh kind. The array form ([x,y,z], applied in the mesh's OWN authoring axes BEFORE rotation) always existed and is now documented. ALSO REPAIRED HERE: the request is kept in RenderObject::size_mul and 'size' derived from it, which makes the substitution idempotent (updateRender3D "mesh" re-runs it, and under a multiplier a second pass over an already-resolved size would square the scale) and fixes a standing defect on that path - a mesh swap now re-scales against the NEW mesh's dimensions instead of carrying the old mesh's metres over verbatim.
//  || 26422 A COMPILE FAILURE MUST NAME ITSELF (full ledger at KH_ENSURE_REPORT). compile_shader has always produced entry point, HRESULT and fxc's own text, and the USER shader paths published it - but the FATAL core ensures (ensure_resources, ensure_composite_shader, ensure_compute_shaders, ensure_effect_shader, ensure_depth_srv, ensure_query_buffers, ensure_fx_chain) RETURN the message and every hot call site tested it with .empty() and threw the string away. Twelve sites. That is 26319 exactly, whose own ledger describes the symptom without naming the cause: gatePassed 0 / compositeSkips 379 / effectSetupFails 0 - three counters naming a starved pipeline while the one string that named the failing shader was constructed, returned and dropped a frame later. kh_ensure_ok routes them into the existing per-session dedupe (one report per distinct message, 256 max), so a shader failing every frame for an hour costs one RPT line, and it returns the emptiness so no control flow moves.
//  || 26419 TWO CLOSURES, ONE BY MEASUREMENT AND ONE BY DECISION. NO BEHAVIOUR CHANGE - ledger only. SECTION 3.6 IS CLOSED. The operator reports the cascade far-edge fade is visible and behaving, which answers the half 26391 could never confirm (that a fade replaced the snap), and 26416 answered the other half by anchoring its width to the engine's own authored crossfade ratio (0.164 of the outermost band's WIDTH) instead of the 0.10 x FAR that its own source called an estimate. 211 still disables it and remains the A/B for either half. THE TELEPORT TRANSIENT IS ACCEPTED, NOT FIXED - full ledger at KH_BAND_TELEPORT_TRANSIENT, and DO NOT RE-OPEN IT AS A BUG. 26418's dump latched one excursion at bandGapCamStep 1610.43 m per frame (a teleport, not motion) where the committed table held slots 2 and 3 as byte duplicates of slots 5 and 6 with nothing covering 18.701-55.039 m; 55.039 - 18.701 = 36.338 = bandGapMaxM. Each slot holds an independently sealed copy of one engine cascade, so a cascade re-layout leaves some slots on the new set and some on the old - a CONTENT SUBSTITUTION at commit, not a registration error (gapCamDx 0.002 and 0.022 on the same frame). NOT EXPLAINED, and recorded as such: that table is not sorted by near edge - slot 4 at 37.644 follows slot 3 at 82.046 - which the commit's finest-first sort should make impossible, so either the sort ran on borders that changed underneath it or the sort is wrong, and nothing distinguishes those. ACCEPTED because it lasts one frame, needs a teleport to provoke, has never produced a reported artifact, and the alternative is more moving parts in the commit path where the receive chain's remaining correctness lives. WHAT WOULD RE-OPEN IT: the gap persisting beyond one frame (bandGapFrames rising in a session with few teleports, or a positive bandGapMaxM latched at an ordinary bandGapCamStep rather than the hundreds); the duplicate pattern appearing without a layout change; or a visible artifact at a teleport. THE INSTRUMENT IT WOULD NEED is the raw physical slot table plus the order[] permutation, latched beside the committed one - one teleport dump separates "the sort was right and the data moved under it" from "the sort is wrong". MODE 85 is the only possible mitigation (no reseal cadence can cover a layout change; the live atlas is correct instantly) and stays opt-in and untested - its original purpose, the drift gap, was closed at the source by 26415/26416, so this is now the only reason it exists. FREE NUMBERS: 222 and up; the whitelist range simply ends at 221.
//  || 26418 THE BLACK BOX IS AN UNPOPULATED SUN, CAUGHT WHOLE BY THE LATCH (full ledger above blkZeroSunRefusals; 99 reverts). At the darkest frame, blkShadeT 32.737 s: blkShadeSunR/G/B EXACTLY 0/0/0, blkShadeAmbR/G/B 0/0.0110/0.0354, blkShadeMin 0.0353 against blkShadeMax 9.0132 - a 255x collapse - with blkShadeValid 1 (the fill took the PUBLISHED branch, not the cold path), blkShadeStdSun 0 (the standing probe agrees the sun is zero) and blkShadeProbeAgeS 2.29e-05 (the locator had JUST confirmed, so staleness is out). blkShadeDarkFrames 968 of 2668. THE LATCH IS WHY THIS EXISTS: the trace ring spans ageS 0.01-12.72 s and every row in it is healthy (blkSunLum 17.07-19.92 on 512 of 512) - the event at 32.7 s is twenty seconds older than the oldest row, so no per-row lane could ever have seen it, which is exactly what 26416 concluded when the whole blkDark family read its null value through a live event. WHAT IT IS: a sun of exactly (0,0,0) on all three channels is not a lighting state - no time of day, weather or altitude in this engine produces an identically zero sun colour beside a nonzero ambient. The locator anchored on the altitude lane, the anchor matched, the block was adopted and published, and its sun lanes had not been written yet. The healthy population never approaches it (blkShadeMax 9.0132, per-row blkSunLum minimum 17.07 across the ring), so this is not a threshold inside a population - it is a distinct value only the fault produces, and rule 1.2 has nothing to bite on because the test is an exact zero rather than a bar. WHY IT SELF-HEALS after ~30 s, which the operator's "recovers eventually" has always described: 968 dark frames of 2668 at ~40 flushes/s is about 24 s, at the START of a ~66 s session. THE FIX IS ABSENT-BEATS-WRONG and costs nothing: an exactly-zero sun is REFUSED and the fill takes the branch it already has for the never-published case - flat white sun over scalar ambient with light_amb.w 0, so every downstream consumer knows. A box lit flat for half a minute is a far smaller error than a black one, and it is the same trade 26380's own ledger already makes for the sub-second cold window. blkZeroSunRefusals IS THE ENGAGEMENT LANE - 0 means the gate never fired and any null is void, the lesson 26378, 26403 and 26413 each paid for separately. IT DOES NOT CLAIM every black box is this: it claims THIS event was an exactly-zero sun reaching the shader (measured) and that refusing it cannot make anything worse (construction). A black box recurring with blkZeroSunRefusals climbing means the zero is being refused and the darkness has a second source. CASCADE - 26416's TIGHTENING WORKS AND THE RESIDUAL IS A DIFFERENT FAULT. bandGapFrames falls from 439 of 2804 (15.7%%) to 138 of 2667 (5.2%%), bandBudgetEscapes 7273, and the operator could not provoke the flicker or the offset. The one latched excursion is NOT drift: bandGapCamStep reads 1610.43 m per frame - a teleport, not motion - and the latched table shows slots 2 and 3 holding 55.039-93.620 and 82.046-147.962, which are BYTE DUPLICATES of slots 5 and 6, with nothing at all covering 18.701-55.039. 55.039 - 18.701 = 36.338 = bandGapMaxM. So during a cascade re-layout some slots still hold the old set while others hold the new, and the sort-by-near plus overlap dedupe at commit passes a table with duplicates and a hole. That is a commit-side fault on a teleport transient, not the drift class 26415/26416 closed, and it should not be conflated with it. COST OF THE TIGHTENING, stated: bandStageWaitMaxMs rises 3.73 -> 24.62 and bandStageDropAge 0 -> 6. Bounded and small, but no longer zero - watch it. FREE NUMBERS: none. The next number spent needs a fresh census.
//  || 26417 THE BLACK-BOX INSTRUMENT MOVES OFF THE PUBLISH PATH (full ledger at KH_BLK_SHADE_LATCH). PURE GAUGE - no gate, no threshold, no arbitration, mode 0 byte-identical. 26416 established that the black box is not a dark publish: a live event with every blkDark* lane at its null value, and blkAcceptRatioMin 0.334956 (a 2.985x collapse on an ORDINARY publish) sitting DEEPER than the 2.79x event, so no ratio bar can separate the classes and section 3.4's plan is falsified rather than pending. This measures what the SHADER WAS HANDED instead - the actual bytes in the frame CB the box shades from, read at the fill: shade = max(sun.rgb) + max(amb.rgb), every frame, ungated. A gauge with a gate in it is how blkDarkPubRefusals came to read 0 through a live event. THE LATCH IS THE POINT, and it is the 26414 band-table pattern that closed the cascade in one dump: the event is rare and short, so a session total or last-frame snapshot will never contain it, and everything is captured AT THE DARKEST FRAME and kept - the block's own rgb (blkShadeAmbR/G/B, blkShadeSunR/G/B), whether the fill took the published branch or the flat-white cold path (blkShadeValid), the lit gate (blkShadeLitGate), the shadow strength (blkShadeStrength), the locator's staleness (blkShadeProbeAgeS), the timestamp (blkShadeT), and the pair that decides it: the STANDING probe luminances at that same instant (blkShadeStdAmb / blkShadeStdSun). blkShadeDarkFrames counts frames under a tenth of the session's own brightest - self-anchored, so it spends no constant and cannot be tuned into agreeing with anybody. PRE-REGISTERED READING, and the three branches name three different subsystems: blkShadeMin near 0 with the standing pair HEALTHY puts the fault between the probe and this fill, i.e. in the g_pub_block_* snapshot or its publish gate, with blkShadeValid saying which branch the fill even took; blkShadeMin near 0 with the standing pair ALSO near 0 puts it upstream in the locator, with blkShadeProbeAgeS saying whether it had simply gone stale; blkShadeMin HEALTHY through an event EXONERATES the lighting block outright and moves the darkness downstream to the shadow terms, the lit gate or the material path - which would retire the whole blk family as the wrong axis, and after seven campaigns aimed at it that is worth more than another gate. FREE NUMBERS: 99.
//  || 26416 FOUR RESULTS: THE CASCADE FOLD WORKS AND IS TIGHTENED, THE FADE WIDTH IS ANCHORED, 213 IS RETIRED, AND THE BLACK-BOX PLAN IS FALSIFIED BY ITS OWN NUMBER. CASCADE - 26415 ENGAGED AND DID WHAT IT WAS BUILT FOR. bandBudgetEscapes 5192 over 2804 flushes, and the latched worst-gap frame shows slots 0-6 COHERENT at camDx 37.8077 identical to six figures. The operator reports no flicker. The residual is slot 7 alone (camDx 0.0142): bandGapPair 6, bandGapMaxM 18.0187, at bandGapCamStep 9.52 m per frame - slot 6 holds [82.046,147.962]-37.808 = [44.24,110.15], slot 7 holds [128.187,248.567]-0.014 = [128.17,248.55], and 128.17-110.15 = 18.02 = bandGapMaxM. MY 26415 LEDGER CLAIMED "bounding each side bounds the difference" and that only holds if the SUM of two adjacent budgets fits inside their overlap. It does not at 0.15: overlap/(w_i + w_i+1) across the eight authored pairs is 0.1320/0.1372/0.1332/0.1257/0.1175/0.1108/0.1062, so EVERY pair fails at 0.15 - by 1.1x at the near end and 1.4x at pair 6/7, which is the one that showed. 0.045 is half the tightest measured ratio with 15%% margin on the pair sum, so every pair holds even with both sides drifting opposite ways at once. The near and mid pairs - where every reported artifact has lived - were already closed at 26415; this closes the far pair instead of leaving a hole that only distance was hiding. Cost stays capped by the 0.05 s near-tier floor (20 Hz per band); 26415 measured the headroom at bandStageWaitMaxMs 3.73 with PoolFall == PoolMiss 1901 and DropAge/DropDead 0. 88 reverts to 0.15. THE FADE WIDTH IS ANCHORED (section 3.6 closed). The band census supplies what 26391 never had: the engine authors its own crossfades at 0.164 to 0.300 of a band's OWN WIDTH, so 0.164 x width is this engine's own measured scale for an invisible cascade transition. 0.10 x FAR scaled with the wrong quantity - the same error 195's threshold made. For the measured table it barely moves (24.86 m to 19.74 m), which IS the findin