// 26415/26416 closed. bandGapCamStep read 1610.43 m per frame - a teleport,
// respawn or fast-travel, not motion - and the committed table read:
//     slot 0    0.000 -   9.481
//     slot 1    6.637 -  18.701
//     slot 2   55.039 -  93.620   <- byte duplicate of slot 5
//     slot 3   82.046 - 147.962   <- byte duplicate of slot 6
//     slot 4   37.644 -  62.494
//     slot 5   55.039 -  93.620
//     slot 6   82.046 - 147.962
//     slot 7  128.187 - 248.567
// Nothing covers 18.701 to 55.039, and 55.039 - 18.701 = 36.338 = bandGapMaxM.
// Each slot holds an independently sealed copy of one engine cascade, so when
// the engine re-lays-out its cascades some slots have already resealed with
// the new set while others still hold the old - two slots land on the same
// band and two bands go missing. The camera drift is irrelevant here
// (gapCamDx 0.002 and 0.022): this is a CONTENT SUBSTITUTION at commit, not a
// registration error.
//
// THE PART THAT IS NOT EXPLAINED, stated so nobody later mistakes this ledger
// for a full diagnosis: that table is NOT SORTED BY NEAR EDGE. Slot 4
// (37.644) follows slot 3 (82.046), and the commit sorts finest-first before
// filling, so a non-monotonic result should be impossible. Either the sort ran
// on borders that changed underneath it (a reseal landing mid-loop) or the
// sort is wrong. Those are different bugs with different fixes and nothing
// here distinguishes them.
//
// WHY IT IS ACCEPTED. It lasts one frame, it needs a teleport to provoke, the
// operator judges a single-frame cascade gap on a screen that is already
// changing completely to be beneath notice, and the alternative is more moving
// parts in the commit path - which is where the receive chain's remaining
// correctness lives. That is a good trade and it is the operator's to make.
//
// WHAT WOULD RE-OPEN IT (so this is a decision, not an amnesty):
//  - the gap persisting beyond one frame, i.e. bandGapFrames rising in a
//    session with few or no teleports, or a positive bandGapMaxM latched at a
//    bandGapCamStep in the ordinary motion range rather than the hundreds;
//  - the duplicate pattern appearing WITHOUT a layout change, which would mean
//    the sort defect is real and fires on its own;
//  - a visible artifact reported at a teleport, which nobody has seen.
// THE INSTRUMENT IT WOULD NEED, if it is ever re-opened: latch the RAW
// physical slot table (unsorted, straight out of g_ls.band[i]) and the order[]
// permutation the sort produced, beside the committed table already latched
// here. One teleport dump then separates "the sort was right and the data
// moved under it" from "the sort is wrong", which is the whole question.
// MODE 85 IS THE MITIGATION IF ONE IS EVER WANTED, and it is the only thing
// that could help: no reseal cadence can cover a layout change, because the
// whole set is being rebuilt, while the live atlas is correct instantly. It
// stays opt-in and untested; its original purpose (the drift gap) was closed
// at the source by 26415/26416, so this is now the only reason it exists.
// ==========================================================================
static float g_band_gap_last_m   = -1.0e9f;  // this frame's worst pair (m)
static float g_band_gap_max_m    = -1.0e9f;  // session worst; > 0 IS a hole
static uint64_t g_band_gap_frames = 0;       // frames carrying at least one hole
// The table AS IT STOOD on the worst-gap frame, latched once and kept.
static float g_band_gap_near[8]  = { -1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1 };
static float g_band_gap_far[8]   = { -1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1 };
static float g_band_gap_age[8]   = { -1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1,-1 };
