// fallback's half-built window is out (26406, mode 214 null) and the
// colour-vs-seam publication gap is out (26406, mode 192 null). A band is
// selected, the CB is full, the gates are open - and the shadow is absent.
// The only thing left is the CONTENT the seal copied, and NOTHING in this
// file has ever reported it. Every closed gauge on the cascade axis compares
// our own state to itself; none of them looks at the texels.
//
// ==========================================================================
// 26410 TWO CORRECTIONS, BOTH MINE, AND THE SECOND ONE INVALIDATES A
// PRE-REGISTERED READING. The operator's first visual-18 capture forced both.
//
// (1) BRIGHTNESS IS NOT A CHANNEL HERE. 26409 carried the pass in brightness
// (full vs 35%%). These returns go out through the HDR composite and the
// TONEMAP, which is precisely the thing brightness does not survive - the
// same pipeline that needs 65504 to force an HDR white elsewhere in this
// file. The capture could not be read for pass at all, and the operator was
// left describing a colour as "dark red ish, kind of brown" with no way to
// tell that from a tonemapped full red. All nine states are SATURATED HUES
// now: pass 0 red / orange / blue / green, pass 1 yellow / cyan / white /
// black, and magenta for no tile at all.
//
// (2) "CLEAR TEXELS" IS THE NORMAL STATE, NOT A FAULT, AND 26409's READING
// TABLE WAS WRONG ABOUT IT. A shadow-map texel holds the depth of the nearest
// caster along the light ray; over open ground with nothing above it there IS
// no caster, so the texel legitimately sits at the clear value and the
// compare correctly returns LIT. The operator's capture is a box on open road
// and it is mostly RED with a clean vertical BLUE strip (a pole's shadow),
// orange along the silhouette edges and a green patch on the top face. That
// is a CORRECT, WORKING receive - real structure, real shadow, in the right
// place - not evidence of anything. 26409 pre-registered "RED/ORANGE means
// the fallback recovered an EMPTY tile, the fault is upstream in the
// capture", and that inference does not hold: red is what an unoccluded
// fragment is SUPPOSED to paint. Struck.
//
// WHAT THE INSTRUMENT ACTUALLY DISCRIMINATES, restated honestly. The signal
// is not which colour dominates - it is WHAT HAPPENS TO THE SHADOWED TEXELS
// (blue, or white under the fallback) AT THE MOMENT OF THE FLICKER:
//   the blue structure VANISHES and its area goes red/yellow -> the content
//     that held the shadow is gone from the tile we are sampling. Capture or
//     seal, upstream of receive.
//   the blue structure MOVES or distorts -> the world->uv mapping shifted.
//     REGISTRATION, and bandPickCamDxM is where it goes.
//   the blue structure STAYS PUT while the screen loses the shadow -> the
//     receive is right and the loss is DOWNSTREAM, in the smf chain or
//     lighting2.w.
//   the box turns MAGENTA -> no tile at all, which is the 26408 gap and the
//     fallback could not close it either.
// Colour ALONE cannot answer this; the before/after of one structure can.
// That is a weaker instrument than 26409 claimed and it is the honest
// description of what a single-frame colour read can carry.
// ==========================================================================
// SUPERSEDED - 26409 THE LEGEND, REBUILT. HUE is the content verdict, BRIGHTNESS is which
// pass answered: FULL = the slab gate selected the band, DIM (35%%) = the
// coverage fallback recovered it after the slab gate found nothing.
//   MAGENTA      no tile holds this fragment at all, either pass
//   RED / maroon      all four taps read the CLEAR value - the tile is empty
//   ORANGE / brown    partly empty - the fragment straddles the edge of what
//                     the engine's atlas actually contained
//   BLUE / navy       real depth, and this fragment compares SHADOWED
//   GREEN / dark      real depth, and this fragment compares LIT
// WHY IT WAS REBUILT: 26408's WHITE only said "the fallback found a tile"
// and stopped, so when the operator reported WHITE during the artifact it
// could not distinguish an EMPTY recovered tile from a real one reading LIT -
// which is the entire remaining question. That was a gauge that answered a
// question adjacent to the one being asked, and it cost a round.
//
// WHAT IT PAINTS, at the fragment's OWN uv in the band the shading path
// would have selected (the same walk, pass 0, no fallback):
//   MAGENTA  no band contains this fragment  (same meaning as visual 14)
//   RED      a band was selected and ALL FOUR taps read the CLEAR value -
//            nothing was ever rasterised into those texels. The seal copied
//            an empty region.
//   ORANGE   partly empty: the fragment straddles the edge of whatever the
//            engine's atlas did contain. This is what a CUT-OFF looks like
//            from inside the shader, and the operator now describes the
//            symptom as a cutoff rather than an offset.
//   BLUE     real depth in the tile, and this fragment compares SHADOWED
//   GREEN    real depth in the tile, and this fragment compares LIT
//
// HOW TO READ IT, pre-registered before the dump so the result cannot be
// rationalised afterwards (rule 1.1):
//   RED or ORANGE on the box during a flicker -> the sealed tile has no
//     content where we are sampling. The fault is UPSTREAM of the receive
//     entirely: either the engine's atlas did not cover that region at seal
//     time, or our copy took the wrong source rect. Next step is the CAPTURE
//     side, not the receive side, and it is the first time this campaign has
//     had a reason to go there.
//   GREEN on the box during a flicker -> the content is real and it says the
//     fragment is LIT. The tile holds a genuine shadow map that does not
//     contain our caster, which means the world->uv mapping lands us
//     somewhere that legitimately has no shadow - a REGISTRATION fault after
//     all, surviving 26403's selection test because selection only asks
//     whether the uv is in BOUNDS, never whether it is in the RIGHT PLACE.
//     That would reopen the seal, and bandPickCamDxM is where it goes.
//   BLUE on the box during a flicker -> the receive says SHADOWED while the
//     screen says lit, so the loss is downstream of ShadowBandFactor: the
//     smf chain (rule 1.15 - enumerate it: band factor, self-shadow min,
//     stencil unit, lit gate) or the strength term lighting2.w.
// Each branch names a different subsystem and they do not overlap. That is
// the point: one read, one direction, no arbitration.
//
// COST: none at mode 0. The function is called from one place, inside the
// visual-18 branch, which is a uniform CB compare. Nothing on the shading
// path references it. It is a deliberate copy of the walk rather than a
// refactor of ShadowBandFactor, for the reason 26395 gives - the hot path in
// both twins is not worth disturbing for an instrument.
// WHY IT RIDES MODE 84: visuals are 0-17 by the historic contract and all
// eighteen slots are spent. 18 already has a CPU reader (26159's dark
// re-seed A/B), so the visual takes a FREE number and the mesh fill
// translates 84 -> dbgCtl.x 18. Visual 17's test was open-ended and would
// have swallowed it; it is bounded at both ends now (rule 1.8).
// ==========================================================================
// ==========================================================================
// KH_STATS_KVF_AUDIT - 26406 TWENTY-NINE METRIC LANES HAVE BEEN PUBLISHING
// TRUNCATED INTEGERS, AND ONE OF THEM IS THE NUMBER SECTION 3.4 IS WAITING ON.
//
// getRenderStats declares TWO publishers a few lines apart: kv(const char*,
// uint64_t) and kvf(const char*, float). Passing a float global to kv()
// compiles silently and truncates. blkAcceptRatioMin is a ratio in roughly
// [0, 3], so it published (uint64_t)0.87 == 0 on EVERY dump since 26379 -
// and its -1.0f sentinel through an unsigned conversion is undefined
// behaviour as well. blkAcceptRatioN read 1885 in the same dump, so the
// accumulator was running correctly the entire time; only the wire was
// broken. Handoff 50 section 3.4 says "get that number, then set the bar
// between it and 2.79. Do not move the bar before it exists." It existed.
//
// WHY IT SURVIVED SEVEN CAMPAIGNS: an integer is a plausible reading for most
// of these. bandPickCamDxMax reported 67, 77 and 27 across three dumps and
// was quoted as metres in two ledgers - all three are suspiciously round
// because all three are floor(). svLiveFovRatio and svLiveFovRatioMax both
// read exactly 1 in every dump on record, which is the rule-1.16 signature
// (a gauge pinned at a constant) hiding behind a value that looks correct
// for a ratio that should be near 1. Only a lane whose true range is BELOW 1
// exposes it, and blkAcceptRatioMin is the first one anybody read.
//
// FIXED at 26406 by routing all 29 through kvf(). Full list, in publish
// order: svSeamRotCamDx, svSeamRotCamDxMax, blkAcceptRatioMin,
// blkAcceptRatioAmb, svCamRtMax, svLiveTrnDx, svLiveTrnDxMax, svSeamNear,
// svLiveTrnAnchOff, svSeamAnchDx, svSeamAnchDxMax, svCbcClassRefNear,
// svLiveFovRatio, svLiveFovRatioMax, volPassWCol, volPassNearLast,
// volDrawNearLast, seamFetchDupM, bandPickCamDxMax, volDrawSwingPx,
// volDrawSwingMax, volPassSwingPx, volPassSwingMax, svSniffOvrRatio,
// svSniffOvrNear, svCompFovRatio, svCompFovRatioMax, svLiveTrnAnchOffMax,
// svGrowLast.
// READ THIS BEFORE RE-USING ANY HISTORIC VALUE OF THOSE LANES. Every figure
// quoted from them in every ledger in this file is a floor(), including the
// 67 m and 86 m band-drift numbers that section 3.3 reasons from. The
// conclusions drawn from them are not necessarily wrong - a floor is still a
// lower bound, which is the safe direction for a MAX lane - but no threshold
// may be anchored to them until they are re-read at 26406 or later.
// PER-ROW TRACE LANES ARE NOT AFFECTED: the trace publishes floats through a
// different path, which is why seamTrnDxM, primeCamDxM and recvBandCamDxM
// have always carried full precision and every fold in this campaign that
