// census says why). dump8 ran the whole session on dbg 23 and the operator
// report is unambiguous: no flicker, but the 26147 look-down hole is back -
// the shadow simply goes away. The lanes name the mechanism exactly:
//   maskSrvBindFrames 2529 / flushes 2532, maskSrvDryFrames 0 - the bind is
//     observable on every frame, so the census go/no-go passed.
//   maskSrvFirstD 0, maskSrvLastD 204 - but EVERY one of the ~30 binds per
//     frame lands in the SCENE phase. topoResolveFirst is 170, topoPpHead
//     171, topoFirstSweep 270, topoLastSweep 354. Not one mask bind occurs
//     after the sweeps.
//   fireFirstDPub 161 == fireLastDPub 161 - so the re-keyed arm put the
//     frame's single fire at draw 161, mid-scene, 109 draws BEFORE this
//     frame's first resolve sweep. The engine then regenerates the mask
//     across 270-354 and our paint is simply overwritten. The look-down
//     hole is not a shader reject and not an epoch - it is a paint that no
//     longer exists by the time anything reads the mask.
// The consequence runs backwards through the whole campaign. If the engine
// only ever binds the mask during the scene phase, then "mask SRV-bound at
// the fire" - measured at draws 230-354, after every bind has happened -
// was never the apply window opening. It is the probability that nothing
// else has taken t15 since the scene phase ended. That is a leftover
// binding, and it is why the number reads 24% (dump7) rather than 0 or 100:
// it is measuring t15 occupancy, not consumption. The 26151 reading, and
// the 26152 clamp default that rests on it, need re-deriving from the
// ordering rather than from that census.
// What is missing to close it is the one ordering fact still unpublished:
// when the engine rebinds the mask as a RENDER TARGET, i.e. when a paint
// stops existing. g_mask_rt_binds_f and g_mask_last_bind_d have tracked it
// per frame since 26133 and have never been emitted outside the flight
// recorder. Published here beside the fire and bind indices, one default-
// mode dump then carries the complete frame: mask RT binds, mask SRV binds,
// resolve sweeps, and our fires, all on the same draw axis.
static uint32_t g_mask_rt_binds_pub = 0;      // published at the boundary, with the rest
static float    g_mask_last_bind_d_pub = -1.0f;

// 26157 PER-FRAME PAINT SIGNAL + RECORDER REPAIR (task 1). The default-mode
// dump_and_trace finally lays the whole frame on one axis, and it reverses
// the 26156 reading of the SRV census - 26151 was right and my "leftover
// binding" objection was wrong:
//   maskSrvFirstD 0, maskSrvLastD 259   - the engine READS the mask across
//     draws 0-259, the scene phase.
//   fireFirstDPub 2                     - and our FIRST fire is at draw 2,
//     INSIDE that read window. That is why fireSrvIndex is 1 and why the
//     census sees the mask bound at fire 1. It is a genuine apply-window
//     sighting, not t15 occupancy left over from earlier.
//   fireLastDPub 511, topoFirstSweep 309, topoLastSweep 511 - fires 2-9 ride
//     the sweeps at the END of the frame, painting for the NEXT frame read.
//   maskRtBindsPub 10, maskLastBindDPub 513 - and the engine binds the mask
//     as a RENDER TARGET ten times per frame, the last at draw 513, i.e.
//     AFTER our last fire at 511.
// Fire 1 is armed by the PUBLISH path, which does not wait for this frame's
// sweep - so it lands at draw 2 carrying the previous frame's sweep inputs,
// and paints straight into the mask the engine is reading right now. The
// backstop paint at fire 9 (draw 511) does not: it has to survive the RT
// bind at 513 and nine more early next frame before anything reads it.
// That is a complete and testable account of the flicker - it should occur
// on backstop frames and only on backstop frames - and it says why look-down
// is where it shows, since that is where SRV sightings collapse and the
// backstop carries the paint.
// It could not be confirmed from this session because the flight recorder
// cannot see any of it. maskRtBinds read 0 and maskLastBindD read -1 on all
// 512 frames, and fireFirstD == fireLastD == 2 on nearly all of them: the
// recorder fills at the flush, early in the frame, where the live per-frame
// lanes are still empty and only fire 1 has happened. Those columns have
// been dead since 26133 and nobody noticed because nothing ever read them.
// 26157 points them at the boundary-published values (a complete frame, one
// frame delayed) and adds the column the flicker actually needs, which no
// instrument has ever carried: WHICH fire took the paint, and by which path.
// firePaintPath 2 on a frame the operator saw blink is the confirmation;
// firePaintPath 1 on such a frame kills the account outright.
static uint32_t g_fire_paint_ix = 0;         // this frame: index of the fire that painted
static uint32_t g_fire_paint_path = 0;       // this frame: 1 SRV-bound, 2 backstop
static uint32_t g_fire_paint_ix_pub = 0;     // published at the boundary with the rest
static uint32_t g_fire_paint_path_pub = 0;
// 26171 THE FALSIFICATION HOOK FOR THE WHOLE FLICKER MODEL, and the only
// number that can reopen it. The account says one visible blink per 1->2
// transition and nothing else: a frame that painted early wiped its own paint
