static uint32_t g_fire_paint_path_prev = 0;
static uint64_t g_fire_flicker_transitions = 0;   // path 1 -> path 2
// 26172 BRIDGE ARM - the flicker fix, and the assumption nobody checked.
// Mode 22 stopping the blinks in the field settles the mechanism: a path-1
// frame wipes its own paint at its sweeps, suppresses its backstop, and the
// following LATE frame reads an empty mask because ITS fire 1 does not land
// until draw ~104 of a 0-135 read window.
// Every attempt to fix that has tried to move the PAINT. The arm was ruled
// untouchable because the publish is late and the engine will not upload the
// view sooner (26167: pubAltHits 0, and that stands). What was never checked
// is whether the fire NEEDS the publish at all. It does not:
//     fireViewBridgeFires 5100      fireViewPubFires 0     (dump101)
//     fireViewBridgeFires 4640      fireViewPubFires 0     (dump99)
// The freeze adopts g_ro.cycle_pv - THE BRIDGE - and has since 26142. The
// published view is read, gated on, and then thrown away. And the depth is
// the previous frame's at draw 2 regardless, because g_mask.cast_depth is
// captured at the resolve sweep at draw 202+; that is precisely what every
// early-mode frame already fires with today, and early frames neither blink
// nor ghost. So every input the fire consumes at draw 2 exists at draw 0.
// THE GATE'S OWN JUSTIFICATION IS OBSOLETE. "Capture-only arming dragged the
// shadow a frame behind the camera" dates from before 26142, when the fire
// painted with g_ls.frame_view: with no publish that WAS last frame's view,
// a frame behind. The bridge runs a frame AHEAD. The failure the gate was
// built to prevent cannot occur through this path any more.
// So: arm on the bridge. Every frame becomes an early frame, paints once,
// same-frame-aimed, covering its own reads - the exact configuration that
// already works on 30-60% of frames and produces neither blink nor double.
// NOT boundary arming. The overcast that killed that is real and belongs to
// PARTITIONED frames, so this arms from the draw path only, keeps
// phase_on_atlas, and keeps every standing guard untouched - including miss
// 51, the 250 ms recent-exact-publish bar, which is the lock-health signal
// the publish gate was really standing in for and is not weakened here.
// EXPECT: fireFirstD collapses to single digits on ~every frame,
// firePaintPath 1 on ~every frame, flickerTransitions 0, castBridgeArms
// large. pubLateFrames is UNCHANGED - the publish is still late, we simply
// stopped waiting for it.
// FALSIFIED IF: castPitchNegHolds/castPitchNegMiss climb, or a look-down
// overcast returns (stale close-range depth MIN-darkening the screen), or
// the shadow starts lagging the camera. setRenderDebug 43 reverts to the
// publish-gated arm exactly as 26171 shipped it.
// 26173 RECEIVE BAND CENSUS (the look-down receive fault).
// Symptom: with the Zeus camera above the box, at STEEP look-down, the world
// shadow received ONTO the box flickers into the wrong position while the
// camera moves, and settles the instant it stops.
// RULED OUT FIRST, so nobody re-derives it: this is NOT a view mismatch
// between the mesh transform and the seal pairing. viewAdoptPreframe 1997
// tracks pubLateFrames 1967 one for one, so the mesh IS drawn with the latch
// on 38% of cycles - but ShadowBandFactor consumes i.wpos, and the vertex
// shader is explicit that "wpos and every shading consumer stay ABSOLUTE and
// byte-identical"; only o.pos takes the rebase. The band lookup is therefore
// independent of whatever camera the mesh was rasterised with. Dead end.
// WHAT IS LEFT is the band SELECTION. The shader walks 8 slots finest-first
// and the FIRST slot whose view-z straddles the point and whose uv/z land in
// bounds wins. Each slot is an independently sealed (vcol, sm, atlas copy)
// triple, and they re-seal at very different rates - band0Copies 1107 and
// band1Copies 1108 against 5143 cycles, i.e. ~21%. A slot is internally
// coherent, so it gives the right answer for the epoch it was sealed in; but
// the engine anchors its cascades to the CAMERA, so as the camera moves a
// world point drifts toward the edge of a sealed extent, fails the uv bounds,
// and the walk falls through to a coarser slot with a different epoch and a
// different resolution. That is a JUMP, it is driven by camera motion, it
// stops dead when motion stops, and steep look-down maximises how much of the
// box straddles a boundary. It fits every part of the report.
// IT IS A HYPOTHESIS AND IT GETS MEASURED, NOT BUILT ON. This census runs the
// shader's own selection rule on the CPU, once a cycle, for the caster centre
// (g_sun_map_bounds - the box), and reports which slot wins, how old its seal
// was, and how often the winner changes.
//   bandPickChanges large and tracking the motion -> confirmed; the repair is
//       at the selection (hysteresis on the winner, or re-sealing on extent
//       exit rather than on content change).
//   bandPickChanges ~0 -> the walk is stable and the fault is INSIDE one
//       slot; read bandPickAgeMaxMs, because then it is seal staleness.
//   bandPickNone climbing -> the point leaves every sealed extent and the
//       receive falls off entirely, which is a third thing again.
static uint32_t g_band_pick = 0;            // winning slot for the caster centre (0 = none)
static uint32_t g_band_pick_prev = 0;
static uint64_t g_band_pick_changes = 0;    // winner flips between cycles
static uint64_t g_band_pick_none = 0;       // cycles no sealed slot contained it
// 26175: bandPickChanges was BLIND to the thing it needed to see. It compares
// pick to pick and only remembers a nonzero winner, so the sequence
//     band 1  ->  NONE  ->  band 1
// registered as no change at all. That sequence is a one-cycle receive
// DROPOUT, and a dropout is exactly what a one-frame flicker looks like.
// dump278 on the correct path: bandPickChanges 0 of 1634 picking cycles - the
// walk never flips, that hypothesis is dead - but bandPickNone 417 of 2051,
// one cycle in five with the box centre inside NO sealed band, and
// bandPickAgeMaxMs 148.56 against a typical 30.6.
// 417 scattered cycles would be a permanent shimmer, not a dozen discrete
// events. 417 cycles in ~a dozen contiguous RUNS is the operator's report
// exactly. These two counters tell those apart, and matching the field count
// is the standard of proof this campaign has run on since firePaintPath.
//   noneRuns ~= the flicker count you see -> the receive dropout IS the fault.
//       Fix at whatever drops the box out: the pack-loop skips (twin dedupe,
//       provisional, insane), or the uv/z bounds, or the seal cadence.
//   noneRuns >> what you see -> the dropouts are invisible and the fault is
//       elsewhere; go at the 148 ms seal age instead.
static uint64_t g_band_pick_none_runs = 0;  // entries into a no-band run
// 26176 PROVISIONAL VCOL AT CONSUMPTION - the last candidate standing, and
// the operator's correction is what named it: the receive does not go ABSENT,
// it goes OFFSET. That kills both earlier readings outright.
//     bandPickChanges   0 of 1634   the band walk never flips
//     bandPickNoneRuns  1 against ~5 flickers seen   not dropouts either
// A wrong POSITION in the RIGHT band means the winning slot's own triple is
// internally incoherent. At capture, sm / border / the atlas copy are written
// together and are coherent. vcol is NOT:
//     const bool fv = frame_view_valid && now - frame_view_time < 0.05f;
//     b.vcol[..] = fv ? g_ls.frame_view[..] : g_ro.cycle_pv.view[..];
//     b.pending_view = true;  b.vcol_bridge = !fv;
// and consumption only refuses the BRIDGE case:
//     if (bs.pending_view && bs.vcol_bridge) continue;
// So a slot with pending_view TRUE and vcol_bridge FALSE is consumed with a
// vcol taken from a publish up to 50 ms old - three frames at 67 fps - while
// its sm came from THIS frame's resolve. wpos -> view -> shadow UV is then
// offset by exactly that camera delta: offset not absent, proportional to
// motion, zero at rest, worst where the vz gradient across the box is largest
// (steep look-down), and transient because the next publish completes the
// seal. Every clause of the report, and bandProvSkips 0 says the guard that
// exists does not cover it.
// MEASURED FIRST. bandPendRuns against the flicker count is the field match;
// bandPendAgeMaxMs is how stale the vcol actually got.
//   bandPendRuns ~= what you see -> confirmed. The repair is to pair vcol
//       with the epoch sm came from instead of with whatever publish is
//       within 50 ms - the same correction 26172 made on the cast side.
//   bandPendRuns ~0 -> wrong again, and the 148 ms seal age is next.
static uint64_t g_band_pend_consumed = 0;   // cycles the winner had a provisional vcol
static uint64_t g_band_pend_runs = 0;       // contiguous runs of those
static uint32_t g_band_pend_len = 0;
static uint32_t g_band_pend_max = 0;
static float    g_band_pend_age_ms = -1.0f; // vcol age of the winner, last cycle
static float    g_band_pend_age_max_ms = 0.0f;
static bool     g_band_pend_this = false;
// 26177 SEAL THE VCOL IN ITS OWN EPOCH - the receive fix.
// dump318 is the field match this campaign runs on: bandPendRuns 8,
// bandPendConsumed 8, bandPendMax 1, against EIGHT flickers reported. Eight
// isolated single-cycle events, and on every one the consumed band's vcol was
// still provisional. bandProvSkips 0, so it was never the bridge case the
// existing guard covers - it was pending_view && !vcol_bridge, which nothing
// has ever refused.
// THE PREMISE THE CAPTURE WAS BUILT ON IS NO LONGER TRUE. Its comment reads
// "DEFERRED VIEW: the main-scene view of THIS frame uploads only after the
// resolves", so it fills vcol provisionally and lets a later publish complete
// the seal. The publish now lands BEFORE the resolves - dump318 topoInject 89,
// topoFirstSweep 340, pubEarlyFrames 1032 of 1095 - so the provisional fill is
// usually already correct, and the deferred completion then overwrites a good
// vcol with a LATER frame's publish while sm stays at the resolve that
// captured it. The pair ends up one frame apart and wpos -> view -> shadow UV
// is offset by exactly that camera step.
// Two defects, one line each:
//   (1) the freshness test is 0.05 s, which is THREE frames at 67 fps, not
//       one. Frame-anchored now against g_boundary_t - the same test
//       kh_adopt_frame_view has always used for the mesh transform.
//   (2) a seal whose vcol is already this frame's view is marked pending
//       anyway, inviting a cross-frame completion. It is COMPLETE at capture
//       now; there is nothing better coming for that sm.
// With no same-frame publish the bridge is taken and also sealed complete:
// g_ro.cycle_pv is this frame's camera, and the freeze's own ledger states
// frame_view[r*4+c] == cycle_pv.view[r][c] elementwise, citing this very
// dual-source fill as the proof. Sub-frame skew against a resolve from the
// same frame, where the old path could be three frames out.
// 26178 FALSIFIED IN THE FIELD, AND IT MADE THINGS WORSE. Reverted to the
// 26176 capture by DEFAULT; setRenderDebug 44 now ENABLES the 26177 form.
// Reported: the receive drifts at ANY angle, in arbitrary directions, under
// camera motion - where 26176 gave eight isolated single-cycle offsets. The
// change converted eight discrete faults into a continuous one, which is the
// signature of a SYSTEMATIC pair mismatch on every seal, not an occasional.
// The error is mine and it is now obvious: sm comes from the ENGINE's resolve,
// so its correct partner is the ENGINE's view for that frame - g_ls.frame_view,
// the published one. g_ro.cycle_pv is the GAME thread snapshot and this file
// states plainly that it "runs a frame AHEAD during movement". Sealing with
// the bridge AND refusing the later completion pairs engine-frame-N sm with
// game-frame-N+1 vcol on every bridge seal - a one-frame mismatch on all of
// them, at every angle, proportional to motion. Exactly the report.
// The deferred completion was NOT the bug. It is the thing that eventually
// puts the ENGINE's own view against the ENGINE's own sm, and I removed it.
// 26172 worked because the CAST paints with the bridge end to end - one
// authority throughout. The RECEIVE pairs against engine matrices, so the
// bridge is the wrong authority here. The lesson does not transfer.
// What survives from 26177: the 0.05 s window is three frames, and eight
// seals were consumed before completion. Any future attempt must KEEP the
// completion and fix WHEN a band becomes consumable - hold the previous
// COMPLETE seal until the new one completes, rather than consuming a
// half-built one. That needs the atlas copy double-buffered; it is a real
// change, not a one-liner. Do not try the bridge shortcut again.
// EXPECT bandPendConsumed and bandPendRuns to go to 0, and the offset with
// them. FALSIFIED IF bandPendRuns stays 8, or bandPickNone climbs by ~8 (the
// offset merely traded for a dropout), or the spawn-window initial-shadow
// drift the bridge guard was built for returns - watch bandSealBridge cold.
static uint64_t g_band_seal_same = 0;     // sealed with THIS frame's publish
static uint64_t g_band_seal_bridge = 0;   // sealed with this frame's bridge
static uint64_t g_band_seal_pending = 0;  // left provisional (26176 behaviour)
// 26179 COMPLETION CENSUS - the instrument 26176/26177 both lacked.
// bandPendConsumed flags the band at CONSUMPTION, which on the model below is
// the one moment nothing is wrong with it. Ordering, dump7 (1657 cycles,
// 70 fps): inject 67, first accept 148, resolves 160, sweeps 235-375, end 377.
// A capture in the sweep tail lands AFTER the cycle's last accept, so it
// crosses the boundary still pending, is consumed at the next injection
// (draw 67) with a COHERENT frame-N sm + frame-N vcol pair, and is then
// completed at draw 148 by frame N+1's publish - which overwrites vcol and
// leaves frame-N sm against frame-N+1 vcol. THAT pair is the offset, it is
// created ~80 draws after the census fires, and it survives until the slot
// reseals (band0Copies 345 over 1657 cycles = every 4.8 cycles, ~68 ms).
// Corroboration already in dump7: bandPendAgeMs 0.61 / max 1.29 is SUB-FRAME.
// Injection sits 2.5 ms past the boundary, so a band pending ~1 ms there was
// captured in the last ~13% of the previous cycle - draw ~330-377 against
// topoLastSweep 375. It matches to the draw.
// The RECEIVE-V2 EPOCH GUARD in the completion loop was built for exactly
// this cross-frame pairing and is set at 0.05 s - THREE AND A HALF FRAMES -
// so a 14 ms boundary crossing sails straight through it.
// PREDICTION, stated before the field round: bandCompleteCross tracks
// bandPendConsumed one for one (~129 in a dump7-shaped session), with
// bandCompleteSame carrying the rest of bandCaptures. FALSIFIED IF
// bandCompleteCross reads ~0 - then the overwrite is intra-cycle, the model
// is wrong, and setRenderDebug 45 must NOT be promoted to default.
// 26179 MODEL FALSIFIED, ON ITS OWN CRITERION. dump99 (2270 cycles):
//     bandCaptures 2957   sealCompletions 2957
//     bandCompleteCross 2957   bandCompleteSame 0
// Not 129 of them - ALL of them. Every completion crosses the boundary,
// always, and the mode-45 field round (dump54, a clean 1490-cycle diff
// window against dump99) was the 26177 report verbatim: the receive drifts
// under motion at ANY angle and snaps right the instant the camera stops.
// Mode 45 never seals the bridge, so the bridge was never the whole story.
// WHAT THE TWO NUMBERS MEAN TOGETHER: OUR CYCLE BOUNDARY - the main-depth
// clear - SITS IN THE MIDDLE OF THE ENGINE'S FRAME. The resolves late in
// our cycle N belong to the engine's frame N+1, whose view uploads early in
// our cycle N+1. So the deferred completion is not a bug that gets lucky,
// it IS the pairing mechanism, and the capture-time comment was right all
