// 26181 DEFERRED CAPTURE - the receive fix, third attempt and the first one
// that does not have to choose.
// The ordering is settled and twice-confirmed: our cycle boundary sits mid
// engine-frame, the resolves late in our cycle N belong to the engine's
// frame N+1, and that frame's view uploads early in our cycle N+1. So the
// deferred COMPLETION is the correct pairing (26179 proved the alternative
// wrong in the field) and the fault has only ever been the WINDOW between a
// capture and its completion, during which the slot held this epoch's sm
// against last epoch's vcol.
// 26180 refused to consume that window and traded the offset for a dropout,
// exactly as its own falsification criterion said it would: dump919
// bandPendRefused 217, bandPendConsumed 0, and bandPickNone 145 -> 421 with
// the run count 2 -> 43. The operator's report tracked it precisely - the
// snap became a disappearance. bandOverlapPairs is high but the overlapping
// twin does NOT contain the caster centre, so refusing the near band leaves
// it uncovered about four cycles out of five.
// The window exists because the capture DESTROYS the previous complete
// triple - sm, border and a fresh CopyResource - a whole cycle before the
// view that completes it arrives. So do not destroy it. Stage the sm and
// border, leave the live triple alone, and commit everything at once when
// the pairing view lands. No half-built state ever exists, no second
// texture is needed (the handoff's double buffer costs 537 MB - eight
// clones of a 4096^2 R32 atlas), and the failure mode is a slightly staler
// but internally COHERENT seal, which is the one outcome this file already
// argues is correct rather than merely tolerable.
// ATLAS DRIFT is measured, not acted on. A staged capture commits its copy
// about a cycle after the resolve that paired it, so the engine may have
// re-rendered a cascade in between (bandStageAtlasMoved counts it). Acting
// on that would risk starving reseals outright if the atlas phase happens
// to sit between the boundary and the first accept, and the exposure is
// small either way: bands are ALREADY 50-250 ms stale between reseals by
// design, against roughly 14 ms of extra drift here. If the counter runs
// high AND the receive looks stale, that is the lane that says so.
// EXPECT bandStageCommits ~= bandStageTakes, bandPendRefused ~0,
// bandPickNone back to its 26179 value (145-ish in a 2270-cycle session,
// essentially all of it one cold run), and neither the snap nor the
// disappearance. FALSIFIED IF bandStageDropAge is a large fraction of
// takes - then the pairing view is not arriving inside the window and the
// double buffer has to be paid for after all.
// setRenderDebug 50 reverts to the 26180 immediate capture (the dropout);
// 48 reverts all the way to 26179 (immediate capture, bridge-only refusal:
// the original offset).
// 26182 THE DEFERRED COPY IS FALSIFIED, AND IT WAS THE FOREIGN SHADOW.
// dump555 (4279 cycles): bandStageAtlasMoved 5633 against 6278 commits.
// NINETY PERCENT of committed bands took their matrices from one atlas
// state and their CONTENT from another, because the engine re-renders
// cascades every frame and the commit lands a cycle after the resolve that
// paired them. The operator saw a shadow of somewhere else entirely, on
// every cold start, never recovering - and it never recovers because the
// mispairing is not a cold-start property at all, it is every reseal.
// I measured this and chose not to act on it, reasoning that ~14 ms of
// drift was small against a receive that is already 50-250 ms stale
// between reseals. That conflated two different things: temporal staleness
// of a COHERENT pair, which is harmless and is what the reseal cadence
// trades in, against INCOHERENCE between matrices and content, which is
// unbounded no matter how small the interval. The counter said 90% and the
// call was wrong.
// The pairing "this sm goes with this atlas content" is only true AT THE
// RESOLVE DRAW. So the copy goes back there, and the previous complete
// triple is preserved the only way left: a BACK BUFFER.
// Every cheap alternative is now falsified in the field:
//   serve the half-built window  -> offset      (26176-26179)
//   refuse the half-built window -> dropout     (26180)
//   defer the copy               -> foreign     (26181)
// What remains is the campaign-28 handoff's own prescription, which was
// right: hold the previous COMPLETE seal until the new one completes.
// COST, stated plainly: the band textures are clones of the engine atlas
// desc - 4096^2 R32, 67 MB each - so a full double buffer is +537 MB. It
// is not needed. Commits run at 6278/4279 = 1.47 per cycle with a one-
// cycle latency, so the number of captures in flight at any moment is
// ~1-2. A shared POOL of two back buffers (+134 MB) covers that, and an
// exhausted pool simply SKIPS the reseal - the slot keeps its old complete
// seal and the next sweep retries, because last_time is not advanced.
// 26187 CORRECTION, and it changes how bandStagePoolMiss reads. The retry
// is NOT 'within ~2 ms'. band_capture runs only from the ~4-draw resolve
// window opened at each atlas-phase exit (~1.3 exits/frame) and each
// qualifying resolve carries ONE cascade, so a given slot gets one attempt
// per FRAME; ~2 ms is the spacing between DIFFERENT bands' sweeps inside a
// cycle, not one slot's retry. Two consequences:
//  - a miss costs a full frame of freshness minimum, ~8x the stated cost;
//  - a blocked reseal SPINS across every frame it stays blocked, and each
//    spin increments BOTH sides of poolMiss / (poolMiss + takes). With
//    holds running to 39.5 ms against a ~17 ms frame that is 2-3 counts per
//    blocked reseal, so dump558's 40.2% is nearer 15-20% of reseals truly
//    deferred. The lane is a dwell-weighted SPIN count, not a failure rate
//    - the bandPickNone trap class landing on this campaign's own newest
//    counter. Size the pool against DISTINCT blocked reseals, which needs a
//    lane counting a miss once per slot per blocked era, before spending
//    the +67 MB.
// TWO IS THE KNEE, and it was measured against the real reseal cadence
// (8 slots, near band 20 Hz, far bands 4 Hz, 8 sweeps a cycle, one cycle of
// commit latency, 300k cycles):
//     pool 1  ->  58.83% of sweep attempts miss   (+67 MB)
//     pool 2  ->   0.11%                          (+134 MB)
//     pool 3  ->   0.05%                          (+201 MB)
//     pool 4  ->   0.03%                          (+268 MB)
// One back buffer is useless and three buys nothing. bandStagePoolMiss is
// the field check on that model; if it runs high, this constant is the only
// thing to change.
// 26394 THE FIELD CHECK THIS LEDGER ASKED FOR HAS RUN, AND IT MISSED BY
// TWO ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE. The model above predicts 0.11% of sweep
// attempts missing at pool 2 and says outright that "bandStagePoolMiss is
// the field check on that model; if it runs high, this constant is the
// only thing to change". Field, over 2222 flushes of look-away/look-back
// and shadow-LOD crossing: bandStagePoolMiss 872 against bandStageTakes
// 2426 - 26% of attempts dropped, roughly 240x the prediction - with
// bandStageWaitMaxMs 38.4 ms. The model assumed ONE cycle of commit
// latency; the field is holding slots for two to four.
// WHY IT MATTERS HERE: a dropped stage is a dropped RESEAL, and the
// big-jump bypass twenty lines below exists precisely so a look-away and
// look-back reseals IMMEDIATELY - its own ledger says the sub-second
// "shadow appears late" was the throttle serving stale windows after a
// view swing. That bypass has been silently defeated ever since: it wins
// its argument with the throttle and then loses the slot. 8 bands wanting
// to reseal at once against 2 slots each held several cycles is a hard
// ceiling of ~2 reseals per cycle, and everything past it is dropped. That
// is the regression the operator remembers - not a change to the bypass,
// but a pool added underneath it.
// CAPACITY 4, COUNT AT RUNTIME. The array is sized 4 but only
// kh_band_pool_n() slots are ever created or searched, so mode 0 pays
// exactly what it pays today (+134 MB total) and 213 pays +268 MB. The
// memory is the whole reason this is an arm and not a fold: 67 MB a slot
// is the operator's call, not this file's. Slots created under 213 are not
// freed on the way back down - they release with the rest of the device
// state - so a session that has ever run 213 keeps the footprint.
// ACCEPTANCE: bandStagePoolMiss collapses toward the modelled fraction AND
// the flick delay goes. If misses collapse and the delay stays, the pool
// was never the ceiling and this reverts.
// 26396 THE DROP WAS THE DELAY, AND MODE 50 PROVED IT. Field: 50 removes
// the flick and the LOD-crossing delay outright. 50 is khdf_staged = false
// - no staging at all - so the difference between 50 and mode 0 is exactly
// what staging does, and on a MISS staging does NOTHING: the capture is
// discarded, the band keeps its old seal, and it retries next cycle. At 32%
// of takes (1017 of 2118, bursty - 0 on 400 rows then 2, 4 or 6, with runs
// of four consecutive miss-frames) that is a third of every reseal thrown
// away, and after a flick all eight bands ask at once so the burst is worst
// exactly when it matters.
// 213 DID NOT TEST THE POOL SIZE. Field on 213: bandStagePoolMiss 286 of
// 461 takes, 38% - no better than pool 2. The slots are only ever CREATED
// inside the atlas-ensure block, which had already run at n = 2, so raising
// the runtime count mid-session found nulls and selected nothing. The arm
// was inert and the pool-size question is still open, not answered. It is
// also no longer urgent: with the fallback below, a miss costs coherence on
// one band for one cycle instead of costing a reseal.
// WHAT THE FALLBACK EXPOSES, stated plainly. Staging exists because three
// cheaper designs were falsified in the field - serve the half-built window
// (offset, 26176-26179), refuse it (dropout, 26180), defer the copy
// (foreign shadow, 26181, at 90% of commits). The immediate capture is the
// 26180 path, so on a miss this band can present a half-built window for a
// cycle. That is the 26180 exposure, restricted from ALWAYS to the ~30% of
// captures that starve - and it buys back a reseal that is currently
// discarded outright. Absent beats stale here: a band serving a one-cycle
// half-built window is closer to the truth than one serving a seal from
// several cycles ago.
// WATCH bandStagePoolFall against bandStagePoolMiss (they should track) and
// watch for the 26180 dropout returning - a band blinking rather than
// lagging. If it does, 214 restores the drop and the answer is more pool,
// with the creation site fixed first so the count actually applies.
static constexpr int KH_BAND_STAGE_POOL = 4;   // capacity; see kh_band_pool_n
// 26416 MODE 213 IS DELISTED RATHER THAN REPAIRED, AND THE REASON IS ITS OWN
// LEDGER. Handoff 50 section 3.7: the arm was INERT because slots are only
// ever CREATED inside the atlas-ensure block, so raising the count
// mid-session finds nulls and selects nothing (bandStagePoolMiss 286 of 461
// takes under 213, no better than pool 2). Two ways out - fix the creation
// site, or delist - and the pool-size question it was built to answer is
// now MOOT: 26396 made a pool miss fall through to the immediate capture,
// and this build's dump reads bandStagePoolFall == bandStagePoolMiss (1901
// == 1901) with bandStageDropAge and bandStageDropDead both 0 and
// bandStageWaitMaxMs 3.73. Every miss is served; a bigger pool would buy
// nothing measurable. Building a creation-site fix to answer a question
// that no longer has a cost attached is how dead machinery accumulates.
// The count stays 2 and the number returns FALSE, so nobody spends another
// session on a switch that cannot move (26383's whole lesson).
// 26458 THE POOL QUESTION IS RE-OPENED BY ITS OWN RE-OPEN CRITERIA, AND
// THE ANSWER IS 4 WITH THE CREATION SITE FIXED (mode 250 reverts to 2).
// dump2 (26455, 32063 flushes, sustained vehicle speed): bandStagePoolMiss
// 18800 of bandStageTakes 31620 - 59% of reseals starved, TEN TIMES the
// 1901 that justified the 26416 delist - with PoolFall == PoolMiss, so
// every one of them was served by the 26396 immediate fallback. That
// fallback is the 26176-class half-built window BY DESIGN (its own ledger
// says so): this epoch's sm sealed against a provisional vcol, i.e. a
// MISREGISTERED band for the frames the pairing publish takes to land.
// At 59% of reseals, under the reseal-demand spike a shadow-view-distance
// entry produces (budget escapes + fresh content on the outer slots),
// that is the operator's "casts land offset for about half a second,
// then settle": the burst of mispaired seals IS the offset, and its
// decay IS the settle. The 26187 knee model priced pool 2 at 0.11% ON A
// ONE-CYCLE COMMIT LATENCY; 26394 measured slots held 2-4 cycles, and at
// that latency the knee moves to 4 (the capacity the array has carried
// since 26394). COST, stated plainly: +134 MB over pool 2 (two more
// 4096^2 R32 clones). The lazy creation at the take site (26458, beside
// the miss counter) closes the 26416 creation-site defect for good: a
// count change now takes effect mid-session, so 250's revert is live the
// moment it is set rather than inert until the next atlas ensure.
// ACCEPTANCE: bandStagePoolMiss collapses by an order of magnitude at
// speed, bandSealSame (the fallback-seal lane) collapses with it, and the
// entry-offset settles instantly; 250 restores the 59% and the offset.
inline int kh_band_pool_n() {
    return g_dbg_mode.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) == 250 ? 2 : 4;
}

struct KhBandStageTex {
    ID3D11Texture2D*          tex = nullptr;
    ID3D11ShaderResourceView* srv = nullptr;
    bool                      busy = false;
};

static KhBandStageTex g_band_pool[KH_BAND_STAGE_POOL];

// Device-object teardown twin for the band textures (atlas desc change and
// device reset both call it). Also clears every slot's staging, because a
// staged capture whose back buffer just died must not commit.
inline void kh_band_pool_release() {
    for (int khbp_k = 0; khbp_k < KH_BAND_STAGE_POOL; ++khbp_k) {
        if (g_band_pool[khbp_k].srv) { g_band_pool[khbp_k].srv->Release(); g_band_pool[khbp_k].srv = nullptr; }
        if (g_band_pool[khbp_k].tex) { g_band_pool[khbp_k].tex->Release(); g_band_pool[khbp_k].tex = nullptr; }
        g_band_pool[khbp_k].busy = false;
    }

    for (int khbp_b = 0; khbp_b < 8; ++khbp_b) {
        g_ls.band[khbp_b].stage_valid = false;
        g_ls.band[khbp_b].stage_pool = -1;
    }

    g_ls.stage_any = false;
}

// 26458: pool slots are created WHEREVER they are first wanted, not only
// in the atlas-ensure block - the 26416 creation-site defect (mode 213
// inert from birth) closed at the root. Desc comes from the live atlas
// exactly as the ensure block builds it; non-fatal, a failed create
// leaves the slot null and the caller's miss path stands.
inline void kh_band_pool_ensure_n(int khbe_want) {
    if (!g_ls.atlas_tex) return;
    if (khbe_want > KH_BAND_STAGE_POOL) khbe_want = KH_BAND_STAGE_POOL;
    bool khbe_missing = false;
    for (int khbe_k = 0; khbe_k < khbe_want; ++khbe_k) {
        if (!g_band_pool[khbe_k].tex) { khbe_missing = true; break; }
    }
    if (!khbe_missing) return;
    D3D11_TEXTURE2D_DESC khbe_td = {};
    g_ls.atlas_tex->GetDesc(&khbe_td);
    khbe_td.BindFlags = D3D11_BIND_SHADER_RESOURCE;
    khbe_td.Usage = D3D11_USAGE_DEFAULT;
    khbe_td.CPUAccessFlags = 0;
    khbe_td.MiscFlags = 0;
    ID3D11Device* khbe_dev = nullptr;
    g_ls.atlas_tex->GetDevice(&khbe_dev);
    if (!khbe_dev) return;
    for (int khbe_k = 0; khbe_k < khbe_want; ++khbe_k) {
        if (g_band_pool[khbe_k].tex) continue;
        if (SUCCEEDED(khbe_dev->CreateTexture2D(&khbe_td, nullptr, &g_band_pool[khbe_k].tex)) &&
            g_band_pool[khbe_k].tex) {
            D3D11_SHADER_RESOURCE_VIEW_DESC khbe_sd = {};
            khbe_sd.Format = DXGI_FORMAT_R32_FLOAT;
            khbe_sd.ViewDimension = D3D11_SRV_DIMENSION_TEXTURE2D;
            khbe_sd.Texture2D.MipLevels = 1;
            if (FAILED(khbe_dev->CreateShaderResourceView(g_band_pool[khbe_k].tex,
                                                          &khbe_sd, &g_band_pool[khbe_k].srv))) {
                g_band_pool[khbe_k].srv = nullptr;
                g_band_pool[khbe_k].tex->Release();
                g_band_pool[khbe_k].tex = nullptr;
            }
        }
        g_band_pool[khbe_k].busy = false;
    }
    khbe_dev->Release();
}

