// 26162 THE OTHER HALF OF THE ORDERING. maskLastBindD has been published since
// 26156 and reads topoDraws on every frame - the engine binds the mask as a
// RENDER TARGET as the last draw of the cycle, two past our last fire, every
// time. That says nothing on its own. What decides whether a paint survives is
// the FIRST such bind of the NEXT frame, because the mask is read across draws
// 0 - maskSrvLastD and rewritten at whichever RT bind comes first. A backstop
// paint lands at the end of frame N and is read early in frame N+1; if frame
// N+1 rebinds the mask as an RT before those reads, that paint never existed.
// Ten RT binds a frame and only the last one has ever been recorded - the
// first is the one that can kill a paint, and it has never been measured.
// 26163 THE FLICKER, MECHANISM AND CURE (dump77 - the first measurement that
// COUNTS CORRECTLY). maskFirstBindD reads 232 and topoFirstSweep reads 232:
// the engine's first mask RT bind IS the first resolve sweep, on every frame,
// and maskFirstBindD > maskSrvLastD on 512 of 512 frames with a minimum
// separation of 53 draws. Reads and writes never overlap. So:
//     reads   draws   0 - 159
//     writes  draws 232 - 360   (sweeps; the last is the final draw)
// A paint after the last sweep is NEVER overwritten before the next frame
// reads it - the 26158 "the backstop is structurally precarious" reading is
// simply wrong, and so is every overwrite theory built on it.
// What IS true falls straight out of the same two numbers:
//   a fire-1 paint lands at draw 2-10 (median 0.7% into the read window), so
//     it covers its OWN frame completely - and the sweeps at 232-358 then wipe
//     it, so it leaves nothing for the next frame.
//   a fire-9 paint lands after the last sweep, so it covers the NEXT frame
//     completely and nothing of its own.
// Each path is complete for exactly one frame, and they cover DIFFERENT
// frames. A run of path-1 frames is fine (each covers itself); a run of path-2
// frames is fine (each covers the next). The failure is the TRANSITION: a
// path-1 frame wipes its own paint at the sweeps and suppresses the backstop,
// so the following frame has nothing in the mask when its reads run at 0-159,
// and if that frame is path-2 its own paint arrives at draw 358 - far too
// late. That frame is dark.
// dump77: FIVE 1->2 transitions in the 512-frame window. The operator reports
// five or six flickers a session. First time in this campaign a count has
// matched, and it matches exactly.
// THE CURE: on a frame that painted at fire 1, do not suppress the backstop -
// paint again at the last fire so the next frame has something to read. Two
// paints, not nine: this is not the 26144 clamp-off. Both replay the frame's
// single frozen epoch (khep_late_used is false on the default path, so no
// re-freeze runs and g_fire_view2 is untouched), the blend is MIN, and the
// only difference between them is that the late one carries this frame's
// swept depth instead of the previous frame's. A second paint can only ADD
// darkness; it cannot remove the shadow, which is the failure being fixed.
// Watch for a smear or a soft double edge under motion - that would be the
// depth difference showing, not a ghost, and it reverts on setRenderDebug 23.
// 26164 THE CARRY WAS ON THE WRONG FRAME. 26163 removed the flicker exactly as
// designed - dump78 still shows six 1->2 transitions and the operator reports
// none of them - but it put a second copy on every path-1 frame instead of on
// the six that needed one, and lateral drift came back with it.
// The arithmetic says why. A carry emitted on frame N is read by frame N+1 at
// draws 0-2302. If frame N+1 also paints at fire 1 (draw ~12), the mask holds
// BOTH during reads 12-2302: frame N's camera and frame N+1's camera, one
// frame apart. MIN keeps both, and under lateral motion they separate
// horizontally. fireCarryPaints 418 against fireSrvClampPaints 419 - a carry
// on essentially every fire-1 frame, so the stale copy was permanent.
// Only six frames a session actually need it, and they are identifiable AFTER
// the fact rather than before: a path-2 frame whose PREDECESSOR was path-1 is
// the one that finds an empty mask, because the predecessor's early paint was
// wiped by its own sweeps and its backstop was suppressed.
// So the extra paint moves off the producing frame and onto the consuming one.
// On a frame that follows a path-1 frame, fire 1 paints as well as the
// backstop. Both paints come from the SAME freeze - fire 1 establishes the
// epoch and every later fire replays it, and khep_late_used is false on the
// default path so no re-freeze intervenes - which means there is no stale copy
// at all this time. The two differ only in depth, as the 26163 pair did, but
// they are the same camera, and they occur on ~6 frames instead of ~327.
// setRenderDebug 23 disables both the prime and the old carry (single-paint
// clamp, the pre-26163 behaviour with the flicker).
static bool     g_fire_paint_was_srv = false;   // this frame: the taken paint was at fire 1
static bool     g_fire_prev_left_gap = false;   // previous frame painted early and wiped it
// 26169 MOTION-GATED CARRY. The flicker's last untested quadrant, and the
// reason it is worth one more build after task 1 closed.
// WHAT TASK 1 ACTUALLY CLOSED is the ARM: pubAltHits 0 and pubRejFamily 0 /
// pubRejCold 0 together prove the engine has not uploaded the view before
// draw ~104-160, so the fire cannot be placed earlier. That kills the whole
// "aim the paint better" family - five of campaign 27's builds. It says
// nothing about how many paints a frame is allowed.
// THE TRADEOFF IS ALREADY FIELD-MEASURED, both halves:
//   single paint (default)  flicker ~7/session   nothing else wrong
//   always carry (26163)    flicker GONE         permanent lateral double
//   prime (26164)           flicker unchanged    no double
// 26163 is the one that matters: the cure WORKED. Six transitions, none
// seen. It was rejected for a side effect, not for failing.
// AND THE SIDE EFFECT HAS A MOTION TERM, in the 26163 ledger's own words:
// frame N's carry is read by frame N+1 alongside frame N+1's own early
// paint - "two cameras one frame apart, MIN keeps both, and LATERAL MOTION
// separates them". The double's visibility is proportional to the one-frame
// camera step. The flicker's is not; it is worst while holding still.
// OPPOSITE MOTION DEPENDENCE. Nobody tested the middle.
// The separation is computable, not guessed, from lanes we already publish:
//     px = camStepM * dimsX / (2 * dist * fovX)
// dump101, camera 3.26 m from the sun-map centre (you stand on top of your
// own shadow):  camStepM 0.0015 -> 0.9 px;  0.005 -> 2.9 px;  0.02 -> 11.8 px.
// So: carry only while the second copy lands inside KH_CARRY_MAX_PX of the
// first. At rest that is a numerical no-op and the flicker is exactly what
// the eye catches there; under motion the carry stands down and the default
// single-paint behaviour returns unchanged.
