// ==========================================================================
// 26254 THE INJECTION IS THE MISPLACED PASS, AND THIS IS WHERE THE CAUSE GETS
// NAMED RATHER THAN GUESSED.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT, one frame, RenderDoc, all three read from the draws' own
// constant buffers:
//   engine volume pass vs engine colour pass   0.0000 px  (ONE view per frame)
//   our COMPOSITE       vs the engine's view   0.0 px     (exact, 6 decimals)
//   our INJECTION       vs the engine's view   21.5 deg = 721 px
// and stenReproj equals the injection draw's own CB byte for byte, so the epoch
// ring is faithful - the injection really did rasterize 21.5 deg away.
//
// WHY THAT IS THE SHADOW LAG AND WHY REPROJECTION CANNOT FIX IT. Our depth
// lands in the volume buffer at screen positions given by the INJECTION's
// matrix; the engine's shadow volumes are rasterized with the ENGINE's. So the
// stencil at texel T answers "is the ray through T, at the depth we wrote,
// inside the caster's volume" - and in the engine's basis that ray passes
// through a DIFFERENT WORLD POINT than our surface. The verdict is
// geometrically wrong, not displaced. Reprojection makes the mesh read our own
// misplaced footprint CONSISTENTLY, which is exactly why it closed the strip
// and exactly why it could never close the other two: it corrects where WE
// think we are, not where the verdict was computed.
//
// AND IT EXPLAINS MODE 107. g_svs_eng_vp is a memcpy of khv_vp_m - the
// INJECTION's matrix, not the engine's. Its "proven equal to the engine's own
// basis x projection" was measured on a SETTLED camera, where the two are
// trivially equal. So 107 draws the visible mesh with the stale matrix and the
// box lags for the SAME REASON the shadows do. The operator reported both
// behaviours and they are one number.
//
// AND svReprojPxMean WAS ALWAYS THIS NUMBER. 21-43 px was read for five builds
// as "the band reprojection corrects". It is the INJECTION'S REGISTRATION ERROR
// AGAINST THE ENGINE. Every ledger that treated it as inherent is wrong.
//
// WHAT IS NOT YET KNOWN, AND IS WHAT THESE LANES ARE FOR. Both passes call
// kh_adopt_frame_view, and the composite lands on the engine's view exactly.
// pubFirstD 22 against topoInject 733 says a fresh view was already published
// long before the injection ran, so "the harvest is late" does not survive
// contact with the dump. Two candidates remain and they need different fixes:
//   (a) adoption is REFUSED at the injection and falls back to cycle_pv or the
//       latch, either of which is a frame away from the engine
//   (b) g_ls.frame_view genuinely MOVES between the seam and the flush
// injAdoptRotDeg is the discriminator. It records the angle between the
// rotation the INJECTION ended up with and the rotation g_ls.frame_view held at
// that instant. Near 0 with svReprojPxMean large means the injection DID adopt
// and frame_view itself moved -> (b). Large means the injection is not on
// frame_view at all -> (a), and injAdoptSrc says which fallback took it.
//
// NO FIX IN THIS BUILD. Four of campaign 35's builds were inference-driven and
// backed out; both that held were single-variable with a stated prediction.
// This is the measurement, and the fix waits for it.
// ==========================================================================
static float    g_inj_adopt_rot_deg = -1.0f;   // |injection rotation - frame_view| (deg)
static float    g_inj_adopt_rot_max = 0.0f;
static uint32_t g_inj_adopt_src = 0;           // 0 = none, 1 = frame_view adopted,
                                               // 2 = cycle_pv, 3 = bridge fetch
static uint64_t g_inj_adopt_fv_cold = 0;       // frame_view absent at the injection
// ==========================================================================
// 26255 WHICH CHANNEL SHOULD THE INJECTION BE ON? The 26254 lanes closed one
// half of the question and opened this one.
//
// WHAT 26254 SETTLED. injAdoptSrc 1 and injAdoptRotMax 0.0198 deg (0.66 px)
// across a whole rotating window: the injection adopts g_ls.frame_view
// FAITHFULLY. So the 37 px mean / 2680 px max gap between the injection and the
// composite is ENTIRELY frame_view moving during the frame - pubAccepts 504840
// over 857 flushes is 589 frame_view writes PER FRAME, and kh_adopt_frame_view's
// own ledger says why: "the sim republishes MID-CYCLE, so under motion that
// value alternates between this frame's and the next frame's camera." That fix
// moved the TRANSLATION onto the latch camera and left the ROTATION on the
// alternating stream. The injection samples that stream early (topo draw 697);
// the composite samples it late and RenderDoc showed the late sample landing on
// the engine's view EXACTLY.
//
// SO THE INJECTION NEEDS A DIFFERENT SOURCE, AND ONLY TWO ARE AVAILABLE AT SEAM
// TIME: frame_view (alternating) and g_ro.cycle_pv (the bridge - "the game-
// thread snapshot [that] runs a frame AHEAD during movement", its own words).
// svSwingPxMean 27 px says those two disagree; it does NOT say which is closer
// to the engine, and magnitudes alone cannot, because cycle_pv may sit either
// between them or on the far side.
//
// injCycleErrPx IS THAT MEASUREMENT. The injection stashes the PRE-ADOPTION
// transform - the one built from cycle_pv alone - and kh_sten_delta_px projects
// the object centre through it as a THIRD arm, against the composite's live
// matrix. The composite is measured equal to the engine, so this reads
// |cycle_pv - engine| in pixels, directly comparable with svReprojPxMean's
// |frame_view(early) - engine|.
//   injCycleErrPx MUCH LESS THAN svReprojPxMean -> the bridge is the better
//     channel and MODE 102's existing revert IS the fix. One line, and the
//     A/B already exists.
//   injCycleErrPx >= svReprojPxMean -> the bridge is no better, both available
//     channels are wrong at seam time, and the injection needs the ENGINE's own
//     view sniffed from its bound constant buffer at the seam. That is real
//     work and it has precedent in this file (shadow_view_scan from the bind
//     path, g_view_scan_from_bind, viewBindScans).
// Either way the fix stops being a guess. STILL NO FIX IN THIS BUILD.
// ==========================================================================
static float    g_svs_cycle_vp[4][4] = {};     // the injection's PRE-adoption transform
static bool     g_svs_cycle_vp_valid = false;
static float    g_inj_cycle_err_px = -1.0f;    // |cycle_pv - the composite's view| (px)
static float    g_inj_cycle_err_max = 0.0f;
static double   g_inj_cycle_err_sum = 0.0;
static uint64_t g_inj_cycle_frames = 0;
// 26256: frames where the three arms violated the triangle inequality. MUST be
// 0. Non-zero means one of svSwingPx / svReprojPx / injCycleErrPx is measuring
// something other than what its name says, and no conclusion may be drawn from
// any of them until it reads 0 again.
static uint64_t g_inj_cycle_incoherent = 0;
// 26258 THE COHERENCE CHECK WAS COMPARING ACROSS FRAMES, and its own reading is
// what said so: injCycleIncoherent 2 in a session where the three arms are
// otherwise consistent on two independent runs. The swing gauge is computed in
// the INJECTION and the delta gauge in the COMPOSITE, and svSwingFrames 1166
// against svInjects 1186 means twenty frames produced no fresh swing sample - so
// on those the triangle test measured this frame's two arms against LAST frame's
// third. That is "a gauge must not gate on state it outlives" (campaign 35) in a
// gauge I added to catch exactly that class of error. The check now fires only
// when the swing sample advanced since the previous evaluation, and a skipped
// check is COUNTED rather than silently passing - an unchecked frame and a
// coherent one must not share a lane.
static uint64_t g_inj_cycle_swing_mark = 0;
static uint64_t g_inj_cycle_unchecked = 0;
// ==========================================================================
// 26257 THE ENGINE-VIEW LOCATOR. STAGE ONE OF TWO, AND IT DELIBERATELY CHANGES
// NOTHING.
//
// WHERE THE CAMPAIGN IS. Three measurements, all byte-level:
//   the engine uses ONE view per frame (volume pass == colour pass, 0.0000 px)
//   our COMPOSITE equals it exactly (0.0 px)
//   our INJECTION is 21.5 deg / 721 px away in the same frame
// and the two channels available at seam time are BOTH wrong:
//   frame_view sampled early   svReprojPxMean   94.9 px
//   cycle_pv (the bridge)      injCycleErrMean 117.6 px
// with injCycleIncoherent 0 and the arms agreeing on frame counts, so those two
// numbers are trustworthy. The operator's mode-102 test said the same thing
// visually before the gauge did. There is no third CPU-side source: the value
// exists in ENGINE memory before its first draw (EID 47 already uses it) and we
// only learn it through the cascade-upload stream, which is 589 writes a frame
// and alternates between this frame's camera and the next.
//
// SO THE VIEW COMES OUT OF THE ENGINE'S OWN CONSTANT BUFFER. Stage two will
// CopySubresourceRegion it GPU-side at the seam and let the injection's vertex
// shader build its transform from it - same frame, no readback, no stall. That
// needs a BYTE OFFSET, and a wrong offset would rasterize our footprint to an
// arbitrary transform: an artifact far worse than the lag AND indistinguishable
// from "the whole approach is wrong". So the offset is located and VALIDATED
// first, and stage two is built on a number rather than on a hope.
//
// THE SEARCH HAS ONE EXACT CRITERION, NOT A HEURISTIC. RenderDoc measured our
// composite EQUAL to the engine's view, so the 4-row block in the engine's CB
// whose 3x3 matches our composite's basis IS the engine's view block. That is
// self-validating - the best-match residual is the acceptance number - and it
// cannot make the mistake an orthonormality search would: EID 47's b2 rows 0-3
// are an IDENTITY WORLD MATRIX, which is orthonormal, and my python
// _find_basis latched onto exactly that and published a 166 deg nonsense
// verdict. One criterion that can be wrong in a measurable way beats three that
// cannot.
//
// THE OFFSET IS LOCATED ONCE AND REUSED, which is the lightLoc pattern this file
// already runs successfully (lightLocOff 40, lightLocFloats 56, lightLocHits
// 4.0e6, lightLocMisses 0, lightLocRelocs 0). Offsets are a property of the
// shader family and do not move; only the values change. So the SLOW structural
// part (finding where, one frame late through a staging Map with DO_NOT_WAIT -
// the cascbind_step pattern) is separated from the FAST part (reading what,
// same frame, stage two). One frame of latency is fatal for the VALUE and
// harmless for the ADDRESS.
//
// 26264 THAT ACCEPTANCE TEST IS RETIRED, and plainly: it never passed. Its lanes
// measured a candidate block against OUR COMPOSITE's basis, and capture 6
// disproved the premise that the composite equals the engine's view. Lanes and
// reference machinery are deleted; the SELF-CONTAINED fingerprint below replaced
// them. That does not fire yet either, for a reason 26263 measured and
// RENDER_HANDOFF_CAMPAIGN_37.md carries forward: the pipeline state bound when
// the stage runs is not the state the RenderDoc capture shows at that draw.
// ==========================================================================
// 26259 THREE DEFECTS IN THE LOCATOR, ALL MINE, ALL PINNED AGAINST THE CAPTURE.
// injEngLocHits 0 / injEngLocMiss 1471 with injEngStageBusy 32 - the staging and
// the readback worked on every frame and the SEARCH found nothing.
//
// (1) TRANSPOSED. I compared the candidate block's COLUMN 0 against the engine's
//     X AXIS. The engine stores its basis as ROWS: at EID 47's b2, float 16 is
//     (0.735421, 0.000000, 0.677610), which IS the X axis, and our composite's
//     col0 equals it to six decimals. Reading khe_f[o + r*4 + 0] took
//     (0.735421, -0.041144, -0.676360) instead - the column - for an error of
//     1.395 against a 0.05 threshold. Correct indexing gives EXACTLY 0.
// (2) WRONG SLOT. The stage picked "the widest" bound CB, and every engine CB
//     reports ByteWidth 65536, so the tie always resolved to slot 0 -
//     injEngLocSlot 0 in the dump - while the view block sat in b2. All four
//     slots are staged and scanned now, which is what cascbind_step already
//     does and what I should have copied instead of improvising.
// (3) EPOCH KEY OFF BY ONE. The stage stamps g_svs_frame_seq at the BRACKET
//     (= N); the composite stamps it after the seam boundary has bumped it
//     (= N+1). So the scan paired the staged copy against the PREVIOUS frame's
//     composite - a whole frame of rotation, tens of pixels, guaranteed miss.
//     Same one-frame shift svVolAgeMax 1 documents, and 26250 already
//     established the right key for it.
//
// Any one of these alone produces 1471 misses, so the three could only be
// separated by pinning each against known-good capture data rather than by
// re-running. THIS IS WHY THE LOCATOR SHIPPED BEFORE THE GPU COPY: with the copy
// layered on top, all three would have presented as one arbitrary footprint
// transform and there would have been nothing to diff against.
static ID3D11Buffer* g_svs_eng_stage[6] = {};      // staging readback, one per VS slot
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_bytes[6] = {};
// 26265: the byte offset the window was taken FROM, per slot. The fingerprint's
// offset is window-relative; adding this gives the ABSOLUTE address in the
// resource, which is the only address that survives the stage draw and the copy
// draw being different shader families. Ledger at g_svs_eng_blk_abs.
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_first[6] = {};
// 26266: the bound numConstants, recorded rather than derived. The staging window
// is min(range, 1024), so window/16 equals the bound range only while the range
// fits - and a mismatch there would make the copy's binding fingerprint
// false-reject every frame on any slot bound wider than 1 KB. The bug that lane
// exists to catch must not be one the lane manufactures.
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_num[6] = {};
static uint8_t  g_svs_eng_stage_pending = 0;       // bitmask
static ID3D11Buffer* g_svs_eng_stage_src[6] = {};  // 26262: AddRef'd sources of the
                                                   // staged copies, so a fingerprint
                                                   // hit one frame later can latch
                                                   // the resource it came from
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_mask = 0;          // 26260: slots staged last time
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_stage_last_bytes = 0;    // 26260: total bytes staged
// ==========================================================================
// 26262 THE ENGINE-VIEW TRANSPORT. State for the same-frame GPU copy.
//
// THE FINGERPRINT IS SELF-CONTAINED, which is the whole reason this build can
// exist. Every earlier locator matched the candidate block against OUR
// COMPOSITE's basis, which needed an epoch pairing between a staged copy and a
// composite fill one frame apart - and that pairing was wrong twice (26257 keyed
// on g_svs_frame_seq, which the seam boundary bumps in between). Worse, the
// reference was not reliable: capture 6 shows our composite 16 degrees out in
// PITCH from the engine in a fast vertical flick, while that same frame's engine
// volume pass and engine colour pass are byte-identical to each other. So the
// composite is not a fixed point to measure against - it is a second patient.
//
// The engine's view buffer identifies itself instead, with no reference at all:
//     rows 0..2   3x3 == IDENTITY, .w column a plausible camera position
//     rows 4..6   3x3 ORTHONORMAL
//     row  7      (0,0,0,1)
// Three independent structural facts on one buffer. No epoch, no pairing, no
// composite - and therefore none of the three failure modes that cost the last
// three sessions.
//
// LATCH THE RESOURCE, NOT THE SLOT. The same buffer is bound at b2 by the
// volume-pass family and b3 by the colour-pass family, so the slot is a property
// of the shader and the resource is the thing that is stable. Each frame the
// copy finds which of the four bound slots holds the latched identity - four
// pointer compares, no readback - and copies 64 bytes from byte 64. Weak
// identity, same discipline as g_svs_mask_cand, and it dies with the device.
// ==========================================================================
static ID3D11Buffer* g_svs_eng_src = nullptr;      // AddRef'd engine view CB
static void*    g_svs_eng_src_id = nullptr;        // weak identity
// ==========================================================================
// 26269 THREE DESTINATIONS, FILLED AT THREE DIFFERENT DRAWS.
//
// The first-person sessions read engVerifyFail 955/955 and 711/711 - every copy
// taken at the seam was a worldView, engVerRotMaxDeg 148.7 - while the sweep kept
// finding gate-8 blocks in those same frames (engFpHits 704). A correct view is
// in that buffer; the seam is not when. Slot 0 is still the seam copy, so the
// shipped behaviour is a strict superset of 26268's; slots 1 and 2 are filled
// from the sweep at whatever draws it lands on, and the vertex shader takes the
// first candidate that passes (ledger at KhEngTry).
//
// SLOT 0 IS DELIBERATELY THE SEAM. If the seam's bytes are good, they are the
// freshest and the most certainly same-frame, so it must be tried first. The
// sweep's slots are same-frame too but taken earlier in the frame, which is the
// price of having them at all.
// ==========================================================================
// 26271: the copy is the WHOLE block now - rotation AND camera. Ledger in the
// HLSL at CBEngView. 240 bytes is the engine resource's own size, measured at
// 26267 (engWideBytes 240), so this takes all of it and the shader indexes.
static constexpr uint32_t KH_SVS_ENG_BLK_BYTES = 240u;
static constexpr int KH_SVS_ENG_CAND = 3;
static ID3D11Buffer* g_svs_eng_cb[KH_SVS_ENG_CAND] = {};   // 64-byte copies, b2/b3/b4
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_cand_copies[KH_SVS_ENG_CAND] = {};   // per-slot fills
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_cand_ctr = 0;                        // sweep slot rotator
static int32_t  g_svs_eng_blk_off = -1;            // byte offset of the ROTATION block
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_copies = 0;              // per-frame copies made
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_copy_skips = 0;          // latched resource not bound
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_copy_fails = 0;          // our CB could not be made
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_binds = 0;               // b2 bind sites that fired
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_fp_hits = 0;             // fingerprint matches
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_fp_miss = 0;             // scans with no match
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_arm_inj = 0;             // published: injection armed
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_arm_comp = 0;            // published: composite armed
// ==========================================================================
// 26265 THE COMPOSITE ARMED ON EXISTENCE, NOT ON FRESHNESS.
//
// The injection arms on khv_eng_ok - the copy's own return value, this frame.
// Both composite sites armed on (g_svs_eng_cb != nullptr), and that buffer is
// created ONCE and lives until the device dies. So from the first successful
// copy onward the composite would arm on EVERY frame, including every frame
// where the copy SKIPPED because the latched resource was not bound
// (engCopySkips) - consuming a stale engine rotation while engArmComp read 1.
//
// A frame-late rotation is the precise defect this whole path exists to fix,
// so an arm that cannot tell fresh from stale would have re-created it inside
// the fix and reported success. One frame of slack is admitted deliberately -
// svVolAgeMax 1 already documents that shift between the injection and the
// late flush - and anything older is refused and counted.
// ==========================================================================
static bool     g_svs_eng_copy_ok = false;         // the LAST copy landed
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_copy_seq = ~0ull;        // g_svs_frame_seq at that copy
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_copy_age_last = 0;       // age consumed at the last arm
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_copy_age_max = 0;        // worst age consumed
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_arm_comp_stale = 0;      // composite arms refused: not fresh
// ==========================================================================
// 26265 RECORD THE ABSOLUTE OFFSET, BECAUSE THE SWEEP MAKES THE STAGE DRAW AND
// THE COPY DRAW DIFFERENT SHADER FAMILIES.
//
// 26262 stored the fingerprint's offset WINDOW-RELATIVE (relative to
// firstConstant * 16 at the staging draw) and the copy rebuilt the address as
// firstConstant * 16 + blk_off, re-reading firstConstant at the COPY draw. That
// is correct only while both draws bind the resource at the same offset. With
// the locator pinned to the seam that was invisible; with the sweep below
// staging from whatever draw it lands on, it is load-bearing.
//
// The absolute offset is the address the bytes that PASSED the fingerprint
// actually had, so it is the one thing measured rather than inferred, and the
// copy uses it. The window-relative value is kept purely so the two can be
// COMPARED at the copy: engOffDisagree non-zero says the engine re-binds that
// resource at a different offset per family, which decides whether a cached
// absolute address can be trusted at all. Zero closes the question.
// ==========================================================================
static int32_t  g_svs_eng_blk_abs = -1;            // ABSOLUTE byte offset in the resource
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_off_disagree = 0;        // window-relative != absolute at the copy
// ==========================================================================
// 26266 THE ADDRESS IS STABLE AND THE CONTENTS ARE NOT, SO THE BINDING GETS
// FINGERPRINTED TOO.
//
// Full autopsy at KhEngRotUsable. In short: the same slot at the same offset
// carries a ROTATION-ONLY block on some frames and a FULL RIGID TRANSFORM on
// others, both perfectly orthonormal with determinant 1, and the copy fired
// unconditionally on the cached address. The shader gate catches it at the point
// of use; this catches the cheap half of it one step earlier and for free.
//
// WHAT IS RECORDED is the whole binding, not just the resource: firstConstant
// and numConstants as they were when the gates PASSED. At the copy site an exact
// match is required. If the two variants are bound through different ranges this
// rejects the wrong one same-frame at the cost of two compares; if they are bound
// identically, engBindRejects stays 0 and that is itself the finding - binding
// metadata cannot discriminate them and the shader gate is the only defence,
// which tells the next build to move the copy rather than tighten this.
//
// IT FAILS CLOSED. A mismatch refuses the copy, so khv_eng_ok is false, so
// neither the injection nor the composite arms and both take the historic path.
// Refusing is never worse than the lag; copying a matrix we have reason to
// distrust is.
// ==========================================================================
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_src_first = 0xFFFFFFFFu;  // firstConstant at the latch
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_src_num = 0;              // numConstants at the latch
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_src_slot = 0xFFFFFFFFu;   // slot at the latch (forensics only)
static uint64_t g_svs_eng_bind_rejects = 0;         // copies refused: binding moved
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_bind_rej_first = 0xFFFFFFFFu;  // what it was instead
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_bind_rej_num = 0;
static uint32_t g_svs_eng_bind_rej_slot = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
